(window.CONTENT_PARTS = window.CONTENT_PARTS || {})["questions-final"] = [{"id":"fq-aw-001","subject":"airway","domain":"GA","codes":["2_GA_I_2"],"note":"complex-airway-ati","refs":[],"status":"draft","stem":"<p>A 60-year-old with a supraglottic tumour and limited mouth opening is to have awake tracheal intubation with a flexible bronchoscope. He weighs 80 kg (lean body weight 60 kg).</p>\n<p>What is the maximum total dose of topical lidocaine commonly recommended for airway topicalisation?</p>","options":["180 mg","300 mg","540 mg","720 mg","No maximum applies to topical use"],"answer":2,"explain":"<p>DAS guidance for awake tracheal intubation suggests a maximum topical lidocaine dose of about <strong>9 mg/kg of lean body weight</strong>: 9 × 60 = <strong>540 mg</strong>. Toxicity from topical lidocaine is possible, especially with repeated doses; monitor and use the lowest effective dose.</p>"},{"id":"fq-aw-002","subject":"airway","domain":"GA","codes":["2_GA_I_6"],"note":"tracheostomy-laryngectomy","refs":[],"status":"draft","stem":"<p>A ward patient with a tracheostomy formed 3 days ago is in respiratory distress. Oxygen has been applied to the face and stoma, and the inner tube removed. A suction catheter will not pass.</p>\n<p>What is the next step in the National Tracheostomy Safety Project algorithm?</p>","options":["Change the tracheostomy tube over a bougie immediately","Deflate the cuff","Give nebulised saline","Intubate orally with a standard-length tube","Replace the inner tube and wait"],"answer":1,"explain":"<p>If a suction catheter will not pass, <strong>deflate the cuff</strong> (the patient may breathe around the tube via the upper airway). If they do not improve, <strong>remove the tracheostomy tube</strong> and oxygenate via the upper airway and/or stoma. With a fresh tracheostomy (&lt; 7 days) the tract is immature and reinsertion is hazardous.</p>"},{"id":"fq-aw-003","subject":"airway","domain":"GA","codes":["2_GA_I_6"],"note":"tracheostomy-laryngectomy","refs":[],"status":"draft","stem":"<p>A patient with a laryngectomy stoma is apnoeic on the ward.</p>\n<p>Where should oxygen and ventilation be applied?</p>","options":["Face mask only","Face mask and stoma simultaneously","Nasal cannulae","Stoma only","Supraglottic airway via the mouth"],"answer":3,"explain":"<p>After laryngectomy the trachea is <strong>disconnected from the upper airway</strong> and ends at the neck stoma: the patient can only be oxygenated and ventilated <strong>via the stoma</strong> (e.g. a paediatric face mask or LMA over the stoma, or intubation of the stoma).</p>"},{"id":"fq-aw-004","subject":"airway","domain":"GA","codes":["2_GA_I_9","2_GA_J"],"note":"ent-laser-jet","refs":[],"status":"draft","stem":"<p>During microlaryngoscopy with supraglottic high-frequency jet ventilation, airway pressure rises and the patient's chest becomes progressively distended, with falling blood pressure.</p>\n<p>What is the most likely cause?</p>","options":["Anaphylaxis","Breath stacking from obstructed expiratory outflow, with barotrauma risk","Endobronchial jet placement improving ventilation","Hypocapnia","Light anaesthesia"],"answer":1,"explain":"<p>Jet ventilation depends on <strong>unobstructed passive exhalation</strong>. If outflow is impeded (laryngospasm, surgical instruments, oedema), gas accumulates, raising intrathoracic pressure (hypotension) and risking <strong>pneumothorax and surgical emphysema</strong>. Stop jetting, allow exhalation, and secure the airway.</p>"},{"id":"fq-aw-005","subject":"airway","domain":"GA","codes":["2_GA_X_4"],"note":"ent-laser-jet","refs":[],"status":"draft","stem":"<p>Why is nitrous oxide usually avoided during tympanoplasty?</p>","options":["It causes excessive bleeding","It diffuses into the middle ear, raising pressure and potentially displacing the graft","It interferes with facial nerve monitoring","It is a trigger for malignant hyperthermia","It reduces middle ear blood flow"],"answer":1,"explain":"<p>N<sub>2</sub>O enters the closed middle ear faster than nitrogen leaves, <strong>increasing middle ear pressure</strong> (and later causing negative pressure when stopped), which can <strong>displace the tympanic graft</strong>. It also increases PONV.</p>"},{"id":"fq-aw-006","subject":"airway","domain":"GA","codes":["2_GA_I_3"],"note":"obstructed-airway-infections","refs":[],"status":"draft","stem":"<p>A 19-year-old with a large anterior mediastinal mass has orthopnoea and stridor when supine. CT shows 70% tracheal compression. He needs a biopsy.</p>\n<p>Which approach is safest?</p>","options":["Biopsy under local anaesthesia (for example of a peripheral lymph node) if feasible","Intravenous induction with rocuronium and supine positioning","Inhalational induction supine, then neuromuscular blockade","Prone positioning under GA with a supraglottic airway","Routine GA because steroids have been started"],"answer":0,"explain":"<p><strong>General anaesthesia can cause catastrophic airway or cardiovascular collapse</strong> with a large mediastinal mass (loss of muscle tone and negative intrathoracic pressure). Obtain tissue <strong>under local anaesthesia</strong> if possible. If GA is essential, keep spontaneous ventilation, have rigid bronchoscopy and ECMO options ready, and be prepared to change position.</p>"},{"id":"fq-aw-007","subject":"airway","domain":"GA","codes":["2_GA_I_11"],"note":"obstructed-airway-infections","refs":[],"status":"draft","stem":"<p>A patient with Ludwig's angina has marked submandibular swelling, trismus and a raised floor of the mouth, but is maintaining their airway with SpO<sub>2</sub> 95% on air.</p>\n<p>Which statement about the trismus is most accurate?</p>","options":["It always resolves after induction of anaesthesia and neuromuscular blockade","It is caused by masseter spasm from suxamethonium","It may be due to mechanical swelling and may not improve after induction","It indicates tetanus","It makes nasal intubation contraindicated"],"answer":2,"explain":"<p>Trismus from pain may improve with anaesthesia, but trismus from <strong>swelling and inflammation</strong> may <strong>not</strong> — after induction the mouth may still not open, and the airway may be lost. Plans such as awake nasal fibreoptic intubation or awake tracheostomy are therefore often chosen.</p>"},{"id":"fq-aw-008","subject":"airway","domain":"GA","codes":["2_GA_I_13","2_SQI_J_1"],"note":"qi-safety-stage2","refs":[],"status":"draft","stem":"<p>After an unexpected difficult intubation that was eventually successful with a video laryngoscope, what is the most important follow-up action?</p>","options":["Document it in the anaesthetic chart only","Inform the patient, document fully, and send a difficult airway alert letter to the patient and GP","Refer the patient to ENT for routine nasendoscopy","Only report it if the patient was harmed","Tell the next anaesthetist verbally"],"answer":1,"explain":"<p>DAS recommends <strong>explaining to the patient</strong>, <strong>documenting</strong> exactly what happened and what worked, issuing an <strong>airway alert letter</strong> (to the patient and GP) and recording an alert in the notes, so that future anaesthetists can plan.</p>"},{"id":"fq-ob-001","subject":"obstetrics","domain":"GA","codes":["2_GA_T_3"],"note":"obstetric-ga-haemorrhage","refs":[],"status":"draft","stem":"<p>A woman with a placental abruption is bleeding heavily. Estimated blood loss is 2,500 mL. Clauss fibrinogen is 1.4 g/L.</p>\n<p>Which intervention is most important for her coagulopathy?</p>","options":["Desmopressin","Fibrinogen replacement (cryoprecipitate or fibrinogen concentrate)","Platelet transfusion alone","Protamine","Vitamin K"],"answer":1,"explain":"<p><strong>Hypofibrinogenaemia</strong> is the key early coagulopathy in obstetric haemorrhage (especially abruption and AFE). A fibrinogen <strong>&lt; 2 g/L</strong> predicts progression to severe bleeding; replace it (guided by Clauss or FIBTEM), alongside tranexamic acid, uterotonics and surgical control.</p>"},{"id":"fq-ob-002","subject":"obstetrics","domain":"GA","codes":["2_GA_T_6"],"note":"pre-eclampsia-hellp","refs":[],"status":"draft","stem":"<p>A woman with severe pre-eclampsia needs a general anaesthetic for category 1 caesarean section.</p>\n<p>Which is the main purpose of giving a drug such as remifentanil or labetalol at induction?</p>","options":["To prevent aspiration","To reduce the risk of intracranial haemorrhage from the hypertensive response to laryngoscopy","To reduce uterine tone","To speed neuromuscular blockade","To treat eclamptic seizures"],"answer":1,"explain":"<p>The <strong>pressor response to laryngoscopy</strong> can cause dangerous surges in blood pressure (intracranial haemorrhage is a leading cause of death in pre-eclampsia). Attenuate it with opioids (remifentanil, alfentanil), labetalol or magnesium.</p>"},{"id":"fq-ob-003","subject":"obstetrics","domain":"GA","codes":["2_GA_T_6"],"note":"pre-eclampsia-hellp","refs":[],"status":"draft","stem":"<p>A woman with pre-eclampsia receiving magnesium sulphate has a caesarean section under general anaesthesia with rocuronium.</p>\n<p>What effect does magnesium have on neuromuscular blockade?</p>","options":["Antagonises rocuronium","No effect","Potentiates and prolongs the block","Prevents sugammadex from working","Speeds recovery"],"answer":2,"explain":"<p>Magnesium reduces prejunctional acetylcholine release (calcium antagonism) and muscle fibre excitability, <strong>potentiating non-depolarising neuromuscular block</strong>. Use quantitative monitoring and reduced doses; sugammadex remains effective.</p>"},{"id":"fq-ob-004","subject":"obstetrics","domain":"GA","codes":["2_GA_T_7","2_POM_T_1"],"note":"obstetric-emergencies","refs":[],"status":"draft","stem":"<p>During a caesarean section, shortly after delivery, a woman suddenly becomes hypoxic and hypotensive, followed by uncontrollable bleeding from all sites.</p>\n<p>What is the most likely diagnosis?</p>","options":["Amniotic fluid embolism","Anaphylaxis to oxytocin","High spinal block","Local anaesthetic toxicity","Venous air embolism"],"answer":0,"explain":"<p>Sudden <strong>cardiorespiratory collapse</strong> followed by <strong>DIC</strong> around delivery is typical of <strong>amniotic fluid embolism</strong>. Management is supportive: CPR, massive transfusion with early fibrinogen, and critical care.</p>"},{"id":"fq-ob-005","subject":"obstetrics","domain":"GA","codes":["2_GA_T_8"],"note":"obstetric-emergencies","refs":[],"status":"draft","stem":"<p>After delivery of the placenta, a woman becomes profoundly hypotensive and bradycardic, with blood loss that seems too small to explain it. The obstetrician cannot palpate the uterine fundus abdominally.</p>\n<p>What is the most likely diagnosis?</p>","options":["Amniotic fluid embolism","Retained placenta","Uterine atony","Uterine inversion","Uterine rupture"],"answer":3,"explain":"<p><strong>Uterine inversion</strong> causes <strong>shock out of proportion to blood loss</strong> (a strong vagal response) and an absent fundus on abdominal palpation. Immediate manual replacement, with uterine relaxation (GTN, volatile), then uterotonics.</p>"},{"id":"fq-ob-006","subject":"obstetrics","domain":"GA","codes":["2_GA_T_14"],"note":"obstetric-analgesia-complications","refs":[],"status":"draft","stem":"<p>A woman who had an accidental dural puncture during epidural insertion develops a severe headache on day 2, worse on sitting. Five days later the headache has become non-postural and she is drowsy.</p>\n<p>What diagnosis must be excluded urgently?</p>","options":["Caffeine withdrawal","Migraine","Subdural haematoma or cerebral venous sinus thrombosis","Tension headache","Uncomplicated post-dural puncture headache"],"answer":2,"explain":"<p>A PDPH that <strong>changes character</strong> (becomes non-postural) or is accompanied by <strong>neurological signs or drowsiness</strong> may indicate <strong>subdural haematoma</strong> (from traction on bridging veins) or <strong>cerebral venous sinus thrombosis</strong>. Urgent imaging and senior review are needed.</p>"},{"id":"fq-ob-007","subject":"obstetrics","domain":"GA","codes":["2_GA_T_2"],"note":"obstetric-analgesia-complications","refs":[],"status":"draft","stem":"<p>Which technique for maintaining epidural labour analgesia is associated with improved spread and less breakthrough pain compared with a continuous infusion?</p>","options":["Continuous infusion of 0.25% bupivacaine","Epidural morphine alone","Intermittent top-ups only by an anaesthetist","Programmed intermittent epidural boluses with patient-controlled boluses","Remifentanil PCA alone"],"answer":3,"explain":"<p><strong>Programmed intermittent epidural boluses</strong> (with PCEA) produce better spread than a continuous infusion, with less breakthrough pain and often lower total local anaesthetic use.</p>"},{"id":"fq-ob-008","subject":"obstetrics","domain":"GA","codes":["2_GA_T_10"],"note":"obstetric-analgesia-complications","refs":[],"status":"draft","stem":"<p>A woman at 24 weeks' gestation needs an urgent appendicectomy.</p>\n<p>Which is the most important additional intraoperative consideration because of her pregnancy?</p>","options":["Avoid all opioids","Avoid neuromuscular blockade","Left uterine displacement and maintenance of uteroplacental perfusion (avoid hypotension, hypoxia and hyperventilation)","Use nitrous oxide to reduce volatile requirement","Routine tocolysis for all cases"],"answer":2,"explain":"<p>After about 20 weeks, <strong>aortocaval compression</strong> must be prevented; maintain <strong>uteroplacental perfusion</strong> by avoiding hypotension, hypoxia and extremes of PaCO<sub>2</sub> (hyperventilation reduces uterine blood flow). Aspiration precautions and appropriate fetal monitoring are also needed.</p>"},{"id":"fq-ob-009","subject":"obstetrics","domain":"GA","codes":["2_GA_T_5"],"note":"obstetric-ga-haemorrhage","refs":[],"status":"draft","stem":"<p>A woman with two previous caesarean sections has an anterior low-lying placenta, and MRI suggests placenta percreta.</p>\n<p>What is the most appropriate planning?</p>","options":["Delivery in a specialist centre with a multidisciplinary team, blood products, cell salvage and planned surgical approach","Emergency caesarean section only when bleeding starts","Spinal anaesthesia at a standard maternity unit with routine preparation","Vaginal delivery with an epidural","Delivery at term in a midwife-led unit"],"answer":0,"explain":"<p><strong>Placenta accreta spectrum</strong> (especially percreta) carries a high risk of <strong>massive haemorrhage</strong> and hysterectomy. Plan <strong>elective delivery in a specialist centre</strong> with a multidisciplinary team, blood availability, cell salvage, and interventional radiology options.</p>"},{"id":"fq-ob-010","subject":"obstetrics","domain":"GA","codes":["2_RMD_B_1"],"note":"obstetric-emergencies","refs":[],"status":"draft","stem":"<p>Which statement about recent MBRRACE-UK maternal mortality reports is correct?</p>","options":["Anaesthesia is the leading direct cause of maternal death","Maternal mortality is equal across ethnic groups","There are persistent inequalities, with higher mortality among Black and Asian women and those in deprived areas","Deaths from thromboembolism no longer occur","Psychiatric causes are not counted"],"answer":2,"explain":"<p>MBRRACE-UK reports consistently show <strong>inequalities in maternal mortality</strong> by ethnicity and deprivation. Leading causes include thromboembolism, cardiac disease and psychiatric causes; anaesthetic deaths are rare.</p>"},{"id":"fq-ct-001","subject":"cardiothoracic","domain":"GA","codes":["2_GA_P_4"],"note":"cardiac-surgery-cpb","refs":[],"status":"draft","stem":"<p>Before initiating cardiopulmonary bypass, heparin is given.</p>\n<p>Which activated clotting time (ACT) is typically required before bypass starts?</p>","options":["120–150 s","180–200 s","250 s","Greater than about 400–480 s","Less than 100 s"],"answer":3,"explain":"<p>A heparin dose of about 300–400 units/kg is given to achieve an <strong>ACT above about 400–480 seconds</strong> (depending on local protocols) before bypass. Normal ACT is about 100–140 s.</p>"},{"id":"fq-ct-002","subject":"cardiothoracic","domain":"GA","codes":["2_GA_P_2"],"note":"cardiac-surgery-cpb","refs":[],"status":"draft","stem":"<p>Three hours after CABG, a patient becomes hypotensive and tachycardic. CVP has risen from 8 to 18 mmHg, chest drain output has abruptly fallen, and the urine output is low.</p>\n<p>What is the most likely diagnosis?</p>","options":["Anaphylaxis to protamine","Cardiac tamponade","Hypovolaemia","Tension pneumothorax on the left","Vasoplegia"],"answer":1,"explain":"<p>Hypotension with <strong>rising filling pressures</strong> and a <strong>sudden fall in drain output</strong> (clotted drains) after cardiac surgery suggests <strong>tamponade</strong>. Echocardiography can help (localised clot may be missed); treatment is <strong>urgent re-exploration</strong>.</p>"},{"id":"fq-ct-003","subject":"cardiothoracic","domain":"GA","codes":["2_GA_O_3"],"note":"cardiac-surgery-cpb","refs":[],"status":"draft","stem":"<p>When should an intra-aortic balloon pump inflate during the cardiac cycle?</p>","options":["At the R wave, before ventricular ejection","During isovolumetric contraction","At the dicrotic notch (aortic valve closure), in early diastole","At peak systole","Continuously throughout the cycle"],"answer":2,"explain":"<p>The IABP <strong>inflates at aortic valve closure</strong> (dicrotic notch) to <strong>augment diastolic coronary perfusion</strong>, and <strong>deflates just before systole</strong> to reduce afterload. It is contraindicated with significant aortic regurgitation.</p>"},{"id":"fq-ct-004","subject":"cardiothoracic","domain":"GA","codes":["2_POM_D_7"],"note":"valve-disease","refs":[],"status":"draft","stem":"<p>A patient with severe aortic stenosis (valve area 0.7 cm<sup>2</sup>) becomes hypotensive (75/40 mmHg) after induction, with a heart rate of 70 in sinus rhythm.</p>\n<p>What is the most appropriate immediate treatment?</p>","options":["Atropine to increase heart rate","Dobutamine infusion","Glyceryl trinitrate to reduce afterload","Phenylephrine or metaraminol to restore systemic vascular resistance","Wait for spontaneous recovery"],"answer":3,"explain":"<p>In severe AS, hypotension reduces coronary perfusion of the hypertrophied LV, causing ischaemia and a downward spiral. Restore <strong>diastolic pressure and SVR</strong> with an <strong>α-agonist</strong>; avoid tachycardia and vasodilators.</p>"},{"id":"fq-ct-005","subject":"cardiothoracic","domain":"GA","codes":["2_POM_D_7"],"note":"valve-disease","refs":[],"status":"draft","stem":"<p>Which haemodynamic goal is most appropriate for a patient with severe mitral stenosis?</p>","options":["Maintain a relatively slow heart rate and avoid increases in pulmonary vascular resistance","Aim for a heart rate above 100 to reduce left atrial volume","Aggressive vasodilation to reduce afterload","High PEEP ventilation","Large fluid boluses to maximise preload"],"answer":0,"explain":"<p>In MS, left ventricular filling across the narrowed valve needs <strong>time</strong>: keep the heart rate <strong>slow</strong> (control AF), maintain preload without overload, and avoid rises in <strong>PVR</strong> (hypoxia, hypercapnia, acidosis, pain), as pulmonary hypertension is common.</p>"},{"id":"fq-ct-006","subject":"cardiothoracic","domain":"GA","codes":["2_GA_R_4"],"note":"thoracic-olv","refs":[],"status":"draft","stem":"<p>During one-lung ventilation in the lateral position for a lobectomy, SpO<sub>2</sub> falls to 86% on FiO<sub>2</sub> 1.0. The double-lumen tube position is confirmed with a bronchoscope.</p>\n<p>What is the most appropriate next step?</p>","options":["Apply CPAP to the non-ventilated lung (after informing the surgeon) or optimise PEEP on the ventilated lung","Increase tidal volume to 12 mL/kg","Start inhaled nitric oxide to the collapsed lung","Start a vasodilator infusion","Switch to 2 MAC isoflurane"],"answer":0,"explain":"<p>After confirming tube position and FiO<sub>2</sub> 1.0, improve oxygenation by <strong>recruitment and PEEP</strong> for the dependent lung and/or <strong>CPAP (or oxygen insufflation) to the non-dependent lung</strong>; intermittent two-lung ventilation is a fallback. Large tidal volumes injure the ventilated lung; vasodilators inhibit HPV.</p>"},{"id":"fq-ct-007","subject":"cardiothoracic","domain":"GA","codes":["2_GA_R_3"],"note":"thoracic-olv","refs":[],"status":"draft","stem":"<p>Why is a left-sided double-lumen tube usually preferred even for right-sided thoracic surgery?</p>","options":["It is easier to pass through the vocal cords","The left main bronchus is shorter","The right upper lobe bronchus arises close to the carina, making a right-sided tube harder to position without obstructing it","The right lung cannot be isolated with a left-sided tube","The left-sided tube has a larger lumen"],"answer":2,"explain":"<p>The <strong>right upper lobe bronchus</strong> arises only about 2–2.5 cm from the carina, so a right-sided DLT's bronchial cuff easily obstructs it. The longer left main bronchus gives a larger margin of safety.</p>"},{"id":"fq-ct-008","subject":"cardiothoracic","domain":"GA","codes":["2_POM_G_1"],"note":"lung-resection-assessment","refs":[],"status":"draft","stem":"<p>A patient's preoperative FEV<sub>1</sub> is 60% predicted. A right upper lobectomy (3 of 19 functional segments) is planned.</p>\n<p>What is the predicted postoperative FEV<sub>1</sub>?</p>","options":["40%","45%","51%","57%","60%"],"answer":2,"explain":"<p>ppoFEV<sub>1</sub> = 60 × (1 − 3/19) = 60 × 16/19 ≈ <strong>51% predicted</strong>. Values between 30% and 60% warrant exercise testing.</p>"},{"id":"fq-ct-009","subject":"cardiothoracic","domain":"GA","codes":["2_GA_O_1","2_GA_K_5"],"note":"cardiac-monitoring-devices","refs":[],"status":"draft","stem":"<p>In which situation is stroke volume variation least reliable as a predictor of fluid responsiveness?</p>","options":["Controlled ventilation with tidal volume 8 mL/kg in sinus rhythm","A patient in atrial fibrillation","A closed chest","Controlled ventilation without spontaneous breaths","Normal intra-abdominal pressure"],"answer":1,"explain":"<p>Dynamic indices require <strong>regular rhythm</strong>, controlled ventilation with adequate tidal volumes (about ≥ 8 mL/kg), no spontaneous breathing effort, and a closed chest. <strong>Arrhythmias such as AF</strong> make beat-to-beat variation uninterpretable.</p>"},{"id":"fq-vs-001","subject":"vascular","domain":"GA","codes":["2_GA_K_10"],"note":"aortic-surgery","refs":[],"status":"draft","stem":"<p>During open infrarenal aortic aneurysm repair, the surgeon is about to remove the aortic cross-clamp.</p>\n<p>What is the most likely immediate haemodynamic effect and appropriate preparation?</p>","options":["Hypertension; deepen anaesthesia","Hypotension from reduced SVR and washout of metabolites; optimise volume, communicate for gradual release and have vasopressors ready","No change in blood pressure","Bradycardia only; give atropine","Hyperkalaemic cardiac arrest in all patients"],"answer":1,"explain":"<p>Unclamping causes <strong>hypotension</strong>: loss of afterload, reactive hyperaemia, and <strong>washout</strong> of acid, potassium and mediators (myocardial depression). Prepare with volume loading, communicate for <strong>gradual release</strong>, and have vasopressors ready; check gases and potassium.</p>"},{"id":"fq-vs-002","subject":"vascular","domain":"GA","codes":["2_GA_M_8"],"note":"aortic-surgery","refs":[],"status":"draft","stem":"<p>A patient undergoing thoracoabdominal aneurysm repair has a lumbar CSF drain inserted.</p>\n<p>What is its main purpose?</p>","options":["To measure intracranial pressure","To prevent post-dural puncture headache","To improve spinal cord perfusion pressure and reduce the risk of paraplegia","To give intrathecal analgesia","To reduce the risk of stroke"],"answer":2,"explain":"<p><strong>Spinal cord perfusion pressure = MAP − CSF pressure</strong>. Draining CSF (keeping pressure about ≤ 10–15 mmHg) while maintaining MAP improves cord perfusion when segmental arteries (e.g. the artery of Adamkiewicz) are compromised.</p>"},{"id":"fq-vs-003","subject":"vascular","domain":"GA","codes":["2_GA_C_5"],"note":"aortic-surgery","refs":[],"status":"draft","stem":"<p>A conscious patient with a ruptured abdominal aortic aneurysm has a blood pressure of 85/50 mmHg in the emergency department.</p>\n<p>What is the most appropriate resuscitation strategy before aortic control?</p>","options":["Aggressive crystalloid to a normal blood pressure","Induction of anaesthesia in the emergency department","Permissive hypotension, maintaining consciousness, with blood products and rapid transfer to theatre or EVAR","Start a GTN infusion","Wait for a CT and cross-matched blood before any intervention"],"answer":2,"explain":"<p><strong>Permissive hypotension</strong> (e.g. a systolic around 70–90 mmHg in a conscious patient) reduces further bleeding until the aorta is controlled; avoid large crystalloid volumes; activate the major haemorrhage protocol; and move rapidly to theatre (or EVAR). Induction is performed with the patient prepped and draped.</p>"},{"id":"fq-vs-004","subject":"vascular","domain":"GA","codes":["2_GA_K_9","2_RA_E_4"],"note":"carotid-surgery","refs":[],"status":"draft","stem":"<p>A carotid endarterectomy is performed under a superficial cervical plexus block with the patient awake.</p>\n<p>What is the main advantage of this technique?</p>","options":["Guaranteed lower stroke rate than GA","Direct monitoring of neurological function during carotid clamping","Protection from myocardial infarction","No need for arterial monitoring","Prevention of cerebral hyperperfusion syndrome"],"answer":1,"explain":"<p>The awake patient provides the best <strong>neurological monitoring</strong> (speech, contralateral hand grip) during clamping, guiding selective shunting. The GALA trial found no overall difference in stroke, MI or death between regional and general anaesthesia.</p>"},{"id":"fq-vs-005","subject":"vascular","domain":"GA","codes":["2_GA_K_9"],"note":"carotid-surgery","refs":[],"status":"draft","stem":"<p>One hour after carotid endarterectomy, a patient in recovery develops neck swelling, stridor and difficulty breathing.</p>\n<p>What is the most important immediate action?</p>","options":["CT scan of the neck","Give nebulised adrenaline and observe","Call for help and open the wound to evacuate the haematoma, preparing to secure the airway","Intubate immediately without opening the wound","Reverse heparin with protamine and wait"],"answer":2,"explain":"<p>An expanding <strong>neck haematoma</strong> can compress the airway and cause oedema. <strong>Open the wound</strong> (remove sutures and clips) to decompress, call for senior anaesthetic and surgical help, and secure the airway (which may still be difficult because of oedema).</p>"},{"id":"fq-vs-006","subject":"vascular","domain":"GA","codes":["2_GA_K_1","2_POM_H_4"],"note":"neuroendocrine-tumours","refs":[],"status":"draft","stem":"<p>A patient with a phaeochromocytoma is being prepared for adrenalectomy.</p>\n<p>What is the correct sequence of preoperative drug treatment?</p>","options":["β-blockade first, then α-blockade","α-blockade first, then β-blockade if needed for tachycardia","Calcium channel blocker only","No preparation; treat hypertension intraoperatively","Simultaneous α- and β-blockade from day 1 with fluid restriction"],"answer":1,"explain":"<p><strong>α-blockade first</strong> (phenoxybenzamine or doxazosin) with liberal fluids and salt to restore volume; add a <strong>β-blocker only after adequate α-blockade</strong> for tachycardia. β-blockade alone risks unopposed α-mediated vasoconstriction and a hypertensive crisis.</p>"},{"id":"fq-vs-007","subject":"vascular","domain":"GA","codes":["2_GA_K_1"],"note":"neuroendocrine-tumours","refs":[],"status":"draft","stem":"<p>During laparotomy for a small bowel carcinoid tumour with liver metastases, the patient develops severe hypotension, flushing and bronchospasm when the tumour is handled.</p>\n<p>What is the most specific drug treatment?</p>","options":["Adrenaline infusion","Dantrolene","Glucagon","Octreotide","Protamine"],"answer":3,"explain":"<p>This is a <strong>carcinoid crisis</strong> from mediator release. <strong>Octreotide</strong> (a somatostatin analogue) inhibits release; use vasopressors such as vasopressin or phenylephrine. Catecholamines (adrenaline) can worsen mediator release in some patients.</p>"},{"id":"fq-vs-008","subject":"vascular","domain":"GA","codes":["2_POM_E_1"],"note":"major-abdominal-surgery","refs":[],"status":"draft","stem":"<p>Which element is part of a typical enhanced recovery pathway for elective colorectal surgery?</p>","options":["Routine long-term nasogastric tubes","Prolonged bed rest","Carbohydrate loading before surgery and early oral feeding after surgery","Fasting from midnight including clear fluids","Routine large-volume fluid loading"],"answer":2,"explain":"<p>ERAS includes <strong>carbohydrate loading</strong>, minimal fasting, avoiding routine drains and nasogastric tubes, balanced fluids, multimodal opioid-sparing analgesia, <strong>early feeding and mobilisation</strong>.</p>"},{"id":"fq-vs-009","subject":"vascular","domain":"GA","codes":["2_GA_K_1"],"note":"major-abdominal-surgery","refs":[],"status":"draft","stem":"<p>During liver resection, the surgeon asks for a low central venous pressure during parenchymal transection.</p>\n<p>What is the main risk of this technique?</p>","options":["Hypercalcaemia","Increased hepatic venous bleeding","Venous air embolism and hypoperfusion of other organs","Pulmonary oedema","Hepatic hyperperfusion"],"answer":2,"explain":"<p>A low CVP reduces <strong>backflow bleeding</strong> from hepatic veins, but open veins at low pressure can entrain <strong>air</strong>, and restricted filling risks <strong>hypoperfusion</strong> (renal injury). Restore volume once transection is complete.</p>"},{"id":"fq-vs-010","subject":"vascular","domain":"GA","codes":["2_POM_F_1","2_POM_N"],"note":"emergency-laparotomy","refs":[],"status":"draft","stem":"<p>According to National Emergency Laparotomy Audit standards, which patient should have a consultant surgeon and consultant anaesthetist present in theatre?</p>","options":["All patients over 50","Any patient with a predicted mortality of 5% or more","Only patients requiring ICU after surgery","Only patients with an ASA grade of V","Only patients operated on at night"],"answer":1,"explain":"<p>NELA standards recommend <strong>consultant surgeon and consultant anaesthetist presence</strong> for patients with a <strong>documented predicted mortality of 5% or more</strong>, along with timely surgery, antibiotics and critical care admission for high-risk patients.</p>"},{"id":"fq-sp-001","subject":"specialties","domain":"GA","codes":["2_GA_S_2"],"note":"ophthalmic-anaesthesia","refs":[],"status":"draft","stem":"<p>A patient with a penetrating eye injury has eaten 2 hours ago and needs urgent surgery under general anaesthesia.</p>\n<p>Which approach best balances the risks?</p>","options":["Awake fibreoptic intubation","Deep extubation to avoid coughing","Inhalational induction without neuromuscular block","Rapid sequence induction with rocuronium (or suxamethonium if needed), avoiding pressure on the eye and coughing","Regional (retrobulbar) block to avoid GA"],"answer":3,"explain":"<p>Protect against <strong>aspiration</strong> with an RSI while avoiding rises in IOP (coughing, straining, pressure on the globe). Rocuronium avoids the small IOP rise of suxamethonium (which has nonetheless been used safely). Needle blocks are contraindicated in open globe injuries; deep extubation is inappropriate with a full stomach.</p>"},{"id":"fq-sp-002","subject":"specialties","domain":"GA","codes":["2_RA_D_2"],"note":"ophthalmic-anaesthesia","refs":[],"status":"draft","stem":"<p>Shortly after a retrobulbar block, a patient becomes unresponsive and apnoeic, with bilateral dilated pupils, while the heart rate is maintained.</p>\n<p>What is the most likely cause?</p>","options":["Anaphylaxis","Brainstem anaesthesia from spread of local anaesthetic along the optic nerve sheath","Oculocardiac reflex","Retrobulbar haemorrhage","Vasovagal syncope"],"answer":1,"explain":"<p>Injection into the optic nerve sheath allows local anaesthetic to track to the <strong>brainstem</strong>, causing apnoea, unconsciousness and sometimes contralateral signs. Support ventilation and circulation until it wears off.</p>"},{"id":"fq-sp-003","subject":"specialties","domain":"GA","codes":["2_GA_K_4"],"note":"specialty-ortho-plastics-urology","refs":[],"status":"draft","stem":"<p>Which haemodynamic target is most appropriate during free flap reconstruction?</p>","options":["Haematocrit above 0.45 to maximise oxygen content","High cardiac output, normothermia and a haematocrit of about 0.30–0.35","Hypothermia to reduce flap metabolism","Low cardiac output to reduce flap oedema","Profound hypotension throughout"],"answer":1,"explain":"<p>Flap perfusion depends on <strong>flow</strong> through small vessels: maintain a <strong>high cardiac output</strong>, vasodilated warm periphery, adequate perfusion pressure, and a moderately reduced <strong>haematocrit (about 0.30–0.35)</strong> to lower viscosity while preserving oxygen content.</p>"},{"id":"fq-sp-004","subject":"specialties","domain":"GA","codes":["2_GA_K_3"],"note":"specialty-ortho-plastics-urology","refs":[],"status":"draft","stem":"<p>Forty minutes into a transurethral resection of the prostate using glycine irrigation under spinal anaesthesia, the patient becomes confused and nauseated, with bradycardia and hypertension. He also complains of blurred vision.</p>\n<p>What is the most likely diagnosis?</p>","options":["High spinal block","Hypoglycaemia","Myocardial infarction","TURP syndrome","Urinary retention"],"answer":3,"explain":"<p><strong>TURP syndrome</strong>: absorption of hypotonic glycine irrigation causes fluid overload (hypertension, bradycardia), <strong>dilutional hyponatraemia</strong> (confusion, seizures) and glycine toxicity (<strong>transient visual disturbance</strong>). Stop surgery, check sodium, restrict fluids, and treat severe hyponatraemia cautiously.</p>"},{"id":"fq-sp-005","subject":"specialties","domain":"GA","codes":["2_GA_L_4"],"note":"remote-ect-mri","refs":[],"status":"draft","stem":"<p>Which induction agent is most likely to shorten seizure duration during electroconvulsive therapy?</p>","options":["Etomidate","Ketamine","Methohexital","Propofol","Remifentanil alone"],"answer":3,"explain":"<p><strong>Propofol</strong> is anticonvulsant and shortens seizure duration (it is still widely used with dose adjustment). Etomidate and ketamine tend to lengthen seizures; methohexital has minimal effect and was the traditional agent.</p>"},{"id":"fq-sp-006","subject":"specialties","domain":"GA","codes":["2_GA_H_5","2_GA_A_3"],"note":"elderly-cognition","refs":[],"status":"draft","stem":"<p>Which medication is most likely to precipitate postoperative delirium in an 84-year-old?</p>","options":["Hyoscine hydrobromide","Ondansetron","Paracetamol","Regular low-dose dexamethasone","Sugammadex"],"answer":0,"explain":"<p><strong>Anticholinergic</strong> drugs (especially centrally acting ones such as hyoscine hydrobromide) and <strong>benzodiazepines</strong> are strongly associated with delirium in older patients and should be avoided.</p>"},{"id":"fq-sp-007","subject":"specialties","domain":"GA","codes":["2_GA_X_3","2_GA_X_1"],"note":"tiva-positioning","refs":[],"status":"draft","stem":"<p>In the fifth National Audit Project (NAP5), which factor was most strongly associated with reports of accidental awareness?</p>","options":["Regional anaesthesia","Spontaneous ventilation without neuromuscular blockade","The use of neuromuscular blocking drugs","Volatile maintenance with end-tidal monitoring","Day-case surgery"],"answer":2,"explain":"<p><strong>Neuromuscular blockade</strong> was present in the great majority of awareness reports, as it removes movement as a sign of inadequate anaesthesia. Other factors included TIVA errors, RSI, obstetrics, cardiac surgery and obesity.</p>"},{"id":"fq-sp-008","subject":"specialties","domain":"GA","codes":["2_GA_G_1"],"note":"tiva-positioning","refs":[],"status":"draft","stem":"<p>After 6 hours of prone spinal surgery with significant blood loss and hypotension, a patient wakes with painless loss of vision in both eyes.</p>\n<p>What is the most likely cause?</p>","options":["Corneal abrasion","Ischaemic optic neuropathy","Pituitary apoplexy","Retinal detachment","Transient cortical blindness from glycine"],"answer":1,"explain":"<p><strong>Ischaemic optic neuropathy</strong> (posterior more than anterior) is the commonest cause of postoperative visual loss after prone spinal surgery, associated with long duration, blood loss, hypotension, anaemia and venous congestion. Central retinal artery occlusion (from direct pressure) is usually unilateral.</p>"},{"id":"fq-pd-001","subject":"paediatrics","domain":"GA","codes":["2_GA_W_6"],"note":"neonatal-anaesthesia","refs":[],"status":"draft","stem":"<p>A 5-week-old boy with pyloric stenosis has had projectile vomiting for 3 days. Blood gas: pH 7.52, HCO<sub>3</sub><sup>−</sup> 34 mmol/L, Na<sup>+</sup> 131 mmol/L, K<sup>+</sup> 2.9 mmol/L, Cl<sup>−</sup> 88 mmol/L.</p>\n<p>What is the most appropriate next step?</p>","options":["Immediate pyloromyotomy to stop the vomiting","Rehydration and correction of electrolytes before surgery","Give sodium bicarbonate","Inhalational induction without a nasogastric tube","Start feeds to correct the alkalosis"],"answer":1,"explain":"<p>Pyloric stenosis is a <strong>medical, not surgical, emergency</strong>. The <strong>hypochloraemic, hypokalaemic metabolic alkalosis</strong> must be corrected (e.g. chloride &gt; 100 mmol/L, bicarbonate &lt; 28 mmol/L) with IV fluids and potassium before surgery, to reduce postoperative apnoea and instability.</p>"},{"id":"fq-pd-002","subject":"paediatrics","domain":"GA","codes":["2_GA_W_7"],"note":"neonatal-anaesthesia","refs":[],"status":"draft","stem":"<p>An infant born at 30 weeks' gestation is now 7 weeks old and needs an inguinal hernia repair.</p>\n<p>Why should this infant be admitted for overnight monitoring after general anaesthesia?</p>","options":["High risk of malignant hyperthermia","Risk of postoperative apnoea","Risk of retinopathy from nitrous oxide","Routine surgical practice","Suxamethonium apnoea"],"answer":1,"explain":"<p>Ex-premature infants below about <strong>60 weeks post-conceptual age</strong> (here 37 weeks) are at risk of <strong>postoperative apnoea</strong> after GA, particularly if anaemic; they need apnoea monitoring overnight.</p>"},{"id":"fq-pd-003","subject":"paediatrics","domain":"GA","codes":["2_GA_U_4"],"note":"paeds-common-surgery","refs":[],"status":"draft","stem":"<p>What type of intravenous maintenance fluid is recommended for most children in hospital according to NICE NG29?</p>","options":["0.18% saline with 4% glucose","0.45% saline alone","5% glucose alone","Isotonic crystalloid (with glucose when needed)","Colloid"],"answer":3,"explain":"<p>NICE NG29 recommends <strong>isotonic</strong> crystalloids (e.g. 0.9% saline with 5% glucose, or balanced solutions) for maintenance. Hypotonic fluids have caused fatal <strong>hyponatraemic encephalopathy</strong>, as ADH is often raised in unwell children.</p>"},{"id":"fq-pd-004","subject":"paediatrics","domain":"GA","codes":["2_GA_U_5"],"note":"paeds-common-surgery","refs":[],"status":"draft","stem":"<p>A 20 kg child has an estimated blood volume of 75 mL/kg. The starting haematocrit is 0.36 and the lowest acceptable haematocrit is 0.24.</p>\n<p>What is the approximate allowable blood loss?</p>","options":["250 mL","375 mL","500 mL","750 mL","1000 mL"],"answer":2,"explain":"<p>EBV = 20 × 75 = 1500 mL. Using the common formula <strong>ABL = EBV × (Hct<sub>start</sub> − Hct<sub>target</sub>) / Hct<sub>start</sub></strong> = 1500 × 0.12 / 0.36 = <strong>500 mL</strong>. (Some versions divide by the average haematocrit, giving a slightly larger figure.) These are estimates to guide monitoring and transfusion decisions, not fixed triggers.</p>"},{"id":"fq-pd-005","subject":"paediatrics","domain":"GA","codes":["2_GA_V_1"],"note":"paeds-complex","refs":[],"status":"draft","stem":"<p>Which feature of Down syndrome is most important to consider when positioning the head for intubation?</p>","options":["Atlantoaxial instability","Large tongue only","Macrocephaly","Short trachea","Subglottic stenosis only"],"answer":0,"explain":"<p><strong>Atlantoaxial instability</strong> (ligamentous laxity) means extreme neck flexion or extension could injure the cord: keep the neck as neutral as possible. Other issues include a large tongue, subglottic stenosis (smaller tube), OSA and congenital heart disease.</p>"},{"id":"fq-pd-006","subject":"paediatrics","domain":"GA","codes":["2_GA_U_6"],"note":"paeds-consent-safeguarding","refs":[],"status":"draft","stem":"<p>A 15-year-old understands the nature, benefits and risks of a proposed operation and consents to it. Her parents disagree.</p>\n<p>What is the legal position in England?</p>","options":["A 15-year-old can never consent","Only a court can decide","She can consent if she is Gillick competent","The operation cannot proceed without both parents' consent","The surgeon decides"],"answer":2,"explain":"<p>A child under 16 who has sufficient understanding and intelligence to understand the treatment fully (<strong>Gillick competent</strong>) can give valid consent. Refusal of treatment by a competent minor is more complex and may be overridden in limited circumstances.</p>"},{"id":"fq-ns-001","subject":"neuro","domain":"GA","codes":["2_GA_M_5"],"note":"neuroanaesthesia-principles","refs":[],"status":"draft","stem":"<p>During a craniotomy for a frontal tumour, the surgeon reports a tense, swollen brain. PaCO<sub>2</sub> is 5.2 kPa, MAP 85 mmHg, and anaesthesia is maintained with 1.3 MAC sevoflurane.</p>\n<p>Which change is most appropriate first?</p>","options":["Add nitrous oxide","Increase sevoflurane to 2 MAC","Switch to propofol TIVA, ensure head-up position with good venous drainage, and consider osmotherapy","Lower the head of the table","Reduce ventilation to raise PaCO<sub>2</sub>"],"answer":2,"explain":"<p>Volatile agents at higher concentrations are <strong>cerebral vasodilators</strong>. Switching to <strong>propofol</strong> reduces CBF and CMRO<sub>2</sub>; ensure <strong>head-up tilt</strong> and unobstructed venous drainage, adequate depth, then consider <strong>mannitol or hypertonic saline</strong> and brief moderate hyperventilation.</p>"},{"id":"fq-ns-002","subject":"neuro","domain":"GA","codes":["2_GA_N_8"],"note":"neurosurgical-procedures","refs":[],"status":"draft","stem":"<p>During a posterior fossa craniotomy in the sitting position, the end-tidal CO<sub>2</sub> falls suddenly from 4.8 to 2.9 kPa with hypotension. The precordial Doppler sound has changed.</p>\n<p>What is the most appropriate first action?</p>","options":["Ask the surgeon to flood the field and lower the head","Give adrenaline 1 mg IV","Increase the volatile concentration","Increase the respiratory rate","Start nitrous oxide"],"answer":0,"explain":"<p><strong>Venous air embolism</strong>: stop further air entry — <strong>flood the surgical field</strong> with saline and <strong>lower the head</strong> (raising venous pressure at the wound), apply jugular compression, give 100% O<sub>2</sub> (stop N<sub>2</sub>O), aspirate via a right atrial catheter and support the circulation.</p>"},{"id":"fq-ns-003","subject":"neuro","domain":"GA","codes":["2_GA_N_5","2_GA_N_7"],"note":"neurosurgical-procedures","refs":[],"status":"draft","stem":"<p>Motor evoked potential monitoring is planned for scoliosis surgery.</p>\n<p>Which anaesthetic technique best preserves the MEP signals?</p>","options":["1.5 MAC desflurane with rocuronium infusion","Nitrous oxide 70% and isoflurane","Propofol and remifentanil TIVA without further neuromuscular blockade after intubation","Sevoflurane 1 MAC with atracurium","Thiopental infusion with vecuronium"],"answer":2,"explain":"<p>MEPs are highly sensitive to <strong>volatile agents and N<sub>2</sub>O</strong> and are abolished by <strong>neuromuscular blockade</strong>. <strong>TIVA (propofol and remifentanil)</strong> without ongoing NMB is standard.</p>"},{"id":"fq-ns-004","subject":"neuro","domain":"GA","codes":["2_RT_G_3","2_GA_F_1"],"note":"tbi-neurocritical","refs":[],"status":"draft","stem":"<p>A ventilated patient with a severe traumatic brain injury has an ICP of 28 mmHg and a MAP of 80 mmHg.</p>\n<p>What is the most appropriate target to restore?</p>","options":["CPP of 40 mmHg to reduce bleeding","CPP of 60–70 mmHg while treating the raised ICP","MAP of 60 mmHg with permissive hypotension","PaCO<sub>2</sub> of 3.0 kPa indefinitely","Temperature of 32°C routinely"],"answer":1,"explain":"<p>The CPP is currently 52 mmHg. Aim for a <strong>CPP of about 60–70 mmHg</strong> (raise MAP with vasopressors) while treating the ICP (sedation, osmotherapy, CSF drainage). Permissive hypotension and prolonged hyperventilation are harmful in TBI; routine hypothermia is not recommended.</p>"},{"id":"fq-ns-005","subject":"neuro","domain":"GA","codes":["2_RT_G_1","2_GA_D_1"],"note":"spinal-injury","refs":[],"status":"draft","stem":"<p>A patient with a C6 spinal cord injury 2 hours ago has a blood pressure of 80/40 mmHg and a heart rate of 48 beats per minute, with warm, dry peripheries.</p>\n<p>What is the most likely cause?</p>","options":["Hypovolaemic shock from occult bleeding","Neurogenic shock","Spinal shock","Tension pneumothorax","Vasovagal syncope"],"answer":1,"explain":"<p><strong>Neurogenic shock</strong> (loss of sympathetic tone above about T6) causes <strong>hypotension with bradycardia</strong> and warm peripheries. Always exclude haemorrhage in trauma. <strong>Spinal shock</strong> refers to loss of reflexes below the injury, not haemodynamics.</p>"},{"id":"fq-ns-006","subject":"neuro","domain":"GA","codes":["2_RT_G_2"],"note":"spinal-injury","refs":[],"status":"draft","stem":"<p>A patient with a T4 complete spinal cord injury (injured 2 years ago) develops a sudden headache, sweating and flushing above the chest, a blood pressure of 220/120 mmHg and a heart rate of 44 during cystoscopy without anaesthesia.</p>\n<p>What is the diagnosis and immediate management?</p>","options":["Anaphylaxis; adrenaline","Autonomic dysreflexia; stop the stimulus, sit up, and give a rapid-acting vasodilator","Malignant hyperthermia; dantrolene","Phaeochromocytoma crisis; phenoxybenzamine","Vasovagal reaction; atropine"],"answer":1,"explain":"<p><strong>Autonomic dysreflexia</strong> (lesions above T6): a stimulus below the level (bladder distension) triggers unopposed sympathetic outflow with reflex bradycardia. Stop the stimulus, sit the patient up, and give a fast-acting vasodilator (e.g. GTN); anaesthesia (GA or spinal) prevents it.</p>"},{"id":"fq-ns-007","subject":"neuro","domain":"GA","codes":["2_IC_C_5","2_GA_N_2"],"note":"tbi-neurocritical","refs":[],"status":"draft","stem":"<p>Six days after coiling of a ruptured aneurysm, a patient with subarachnoid haemorrhage becomes confused with new left arm weakness. Sodium is 132 mmol/L.</p>\n<p>Which complication is most likely?</p>","options":["Delayed cerebral ischaemia due to vasospasm","Hypoglycaemia","Postictal state","Rebleeding","Stroke from coil migration only"],"answer":0,"explain":"<p><strong>Delayed cerebral ischaemia</strong> (vasospasm) typically occurs between <strong>days 4 and 14</strong>. Management includes <strong>nimodipine</strong>, maintaining euvolaemia and adequate blood pressure, correcting sodium, imaging, and endovascular therapy.</p>"},{"id":"fq-rt-001","subject":"trauma","domain":"RT","codes":["2_GA_C_4"],"note":"trauma-haemorrhage","refs":[],"status":"draft","stem":"<p>A 30-year-old with penetrating abdominal trauma and no head injury arrives with a blood pressure of 78/40 mmHg. He is talking.</p>\n<p>What is the most appropriate initial resuscitation strategy?</p>","options":["2 L of crystalloid to restore a normal blood pressure","Permissive hypotension with blood products in balanced ratios, tranexamic acid, and urgent haemorrhage control","Vasopressor infusion to a MAP of 90 mmHg","Colloid infusion then CT","Delay transfusion until laboratory results are available"],"answer":1,"explain":"<p><strong>Damage control resuscitation</strong>: accept a lower blood pressure (e.g. systolic 80–90 mmHg or a radial pulse) until bleeding is controlled (not with TBI), resuscitate with <strong>blood and plasma (and platelets) early</strong>, give <strong>tranexamic acid</strong> within 3 hours, and get to haemorrhage control rapidly.</p>"},{"id":"fq-rt-002","subject":"trauma","domain":"RT","codes":["2_GA_C_4"],"note":"trauma-haemorrhage","refs":[],"status":"draft","stem":"<p>Within what time from injury should tranexamic acid be given to trauma patients with significant bleeding, based on CRASH-2?</p>","options":["1 hour","3 hours","8 hours","12 hours","24 hours"],"answer":1,"explain":"<p>CRASH-2 showed reduced mortality from bleeding when TXA was given <strong>within 3 hours</strong> (greatest benefit within 1 hour); later administration was associated with harm.</p>"},{"id":"fq-rt-003","subject":"trauma","domain":"RT","codes":["2_GA_E_2","2_GA_E_4"],"note":"burns-environmental","refs":[],"status":"draft","stem":"<p>A 70 kg adult has 30% total body surface area burns. The burn occurred at 10:00 and the patient arrives at 12:00.</p>\n<p>Using the Parkland formula, how much crystalloid should be given between 12:00 and 18:00?</p>","options":["2,100 mL","4,200 mL","6,300 mL","8,400 mL","12,600 mL"],"answer":1,"explain":"<p>24-h volume = 4 × 70 × 30 = <strong>8,400 mL</strong>; half (4,200 mL) in the first 8 hours <strong>from the time of the burn</strong> (10:00–18:00). Two hours have passed, so the whole 4,200 mL must be given in the remaining 6 hours (12:00–18:00), then titrated to urine output.</p>"},{"id":"fq-rt-004","subject":"trauma","domain":"RT","codes":["2_GA_E_2"],"note":"burns-environmental","refs":[],"status":"draft","stem":"<p>A patient rescued from a house fire has a lactate of 12 mmol/L, a high mixed venous oxygen saturation and reduced consciousness despite a normal blood pressure. COHb is 18%.</p>\n<p>What additional poisoning should be treated?</p>","options":["Cyanide","Methanol","Organophosphate","Paracetamol","Tricyclic antidepressant"],"answer":0,"explain":"<p><strong>Cyanide</strong> (from burning plastics) blocks cytochrome oxidase: cells cannot use oxygen, causing severe lactic acidosis with high venous saturation. Give <strong>hydroxocobalamin</strong>, alongside high-flow oxygen for CO.</p>"},{"id":"fq-rt-005","subject":"trauma","domain":"RT","codes":["2_GA_E_1"],"note":"burns-environmental","refs":[],"status":"draft","stem":"<p>A patient has taken an overdose of amitriptyline and has a QRS duration of 140 ms with hypotension.</p>\n<p>What is the most appropriate specific treatment?</p>","options":["Amiodarone","Flecainide","Flumazenil","Physostigmine","Sodium bicarbonate"],"answer":4,"explain":"<p>Tricyclics block cardiac sodium channels. <strong>Sodium bicarbonate</strong> (raising pH and sodium load) narrows the QRS and reduces arrhythmias. Avoid class Ia/Ic antiarrhythmics; flumazenil can precipitate seizures.</p>"},{"id":"fq-rt-006","subject":"trauma","domain":"RT","codes":["2_RT_B_1","2_RT_B_2"],"note":"periarrest-resuscitation","refs":[],"status":"draft","stem":"<p>A patient in recovery has a regular narrow-complex tachycardia at 190 beats per minute, blood pressure 118/70 mmHg, no chest pain and is alert. Vagal manoeuvres fail.</p>\n<p>What is the next treatment?</p>","options":["Adenosine 6 mg as a rapid IV bolus with a flush","Amiodarone 300 mg over 5 minutes","Digoxin","Immediate synchronised DC cardioversion","Verapamil and a β-blocker together"],"answer":0,"explain":"<p>A stable <strong>regular narrow-complex tachycardia</strong> (likely AVNRT) is treated with <strong>adenosine</strong> (6, then 12, then 18 mg rapid boluses) after vagal manoeuvres. Cardioversion is for adverse features.</p>"},{"id":"fq-rt-007","subject":"trauma","domain":"RT","codes":["2_RT_B_1","2_RT_B_3"],"note":"periarrest-resuscitation","refs":[],"status":"draft","stem":"<p>A patient has a broad-complex regular tachycardia at 180/min with a blood pressure of 72/40 mmHg and confusion.</p>\n<p>What is the most appropriate treatment?</p>","options":["Adenosine 12 mg","Amiodarone 300 mg over 20 minutes as the only treatment","Synchronised DC cardioversion under sedation or anaesthesia","Unsynchronised shock at 360 J without sedation","Verapamil 5 mg IV"],"answer":2,"explain":"<p>A broad-complex tachycardia (VT until proven otherwise) with <strong>adverse features</strong> (shock, confusion) needs <strong>synchronised cardioversion</strong> (up to three attempts), with sedation or anaesthesia as he is conscious, followed by amiodarone.</p>"},{"id":"fq-rt-008","subject":"trauma","domain":"RT","codes":["2_RT_B_9"],"note":"periarrest-resuscitation","refs":[],"status":"draft","stem":"<p>A patient undergoing prone spinal surgery on a spinal frame develops pulseless VF.</p>\n<p>What is the most appropriate immediate action?</p>","options":["Delay CPR until the patient can be turned supine","Start CPR in the prone position with compressions over the thoracic spine between the scapulae, and defibrillate with posterolaterally placed pads while preparing to turn","Give 1 mg adrenaline and wait 2 minutes","Only give precordial thump","Turn the patient supine immediately before any other action, whatever the delay"],"answer":1,"explain":"<p><strong>Start CPR prone</strong> (compressions between the scapulae, with sternal counter-pressure where possible) and defibrillate with pads placed posterolaterally, while preparing to turn supine when this can be done quickly and safely.</p>"},{"id":"fq-rt-009","subject":"trauma","domain":"RT","codes":["2_RT_F_2","2_RT_F_3"],"note":"transfer-advanced","refs":[],"status":"draft","stem":"<p>A ventilated patient is to be transferred for 2 hours by road. Minute volume is 8 L/min with FiO<sub>2</sub> 0.6, and the transport ventilator uses 1 L/min of driving gas.</p>\n<p>Approximately how much oxygen should be taken, including a 100% reserve?</p>","options":["700 L","1,100 L","1,300 L","1,800 L","2,600 L"],"answer":2,"explain":"<p>Oxygen use ≈ (MV × FiO<sub>2</sub>) + driving gas = (8 × 0.6) + 1 = 5.8 L/min (assuming the ventilator entrains air for the remainder). Over 120 minutes ≈ 700 L. <strong>Doubling for reserve gives about 1,400 L</strong>; the closest option is <strong>1,300 L</strong>. In practice, check the manufacturer's consumption data and carry at least 50–100% extra.</p>"},{"id":"fq-rt-010","subject":"trauma","domain":"RT","codes":["2_RT_E_2"],"note":"trauma-haemorrhage","refs":[],"status":"draft","stem":"<p>After insertion of a chest drain for a traumatic haemothorax, 1,700 mL of blood drains immediately.</p>\n<p>What is the most appropriate next step?</p>","options":["Clamp the drain for 24 hours","Observe; this is expected","Early surgical referral for thoracotomy while resuscitating","Remove the drain","Start thrombolysis"],"answer":2,"explain":"<p>An initial drainage of more than about <strong>1,500 mL</strong> (or ongoing losses of about 200 mL/h) suggests major intrathoracic bleeding that usually needs <strong>thoracotomy</strong>; resuscitate with blood products meanwhile.</p>"},{"id":"fq-icm-001","subject":"icm","domain":"ICM","codes":["2_IC_C_3"],"note":"ards-ventilation","refs":[],"status":"draft","stem":"<p>A ventilated patient with ARDS weighs 100 kg; his predicted body weight is 70 kg.</p>\n<p>What tidal volume is recommended for lung-protective ventilation?</p>","options":["300 mL","420 mL","600 mL","700 mL","800 mL"],"answer":1,"explain":"<p>Tidal volume is based on <strong>predicted (ideal) body weight</strong>, not actual weight: 6 mL/kg × 70 kg = <strong>420 mL</strong>, keeping plateau pressure ≤ 30 cmH<sub>2</sub>O.</p>"},{"id":"fq-icm-002","subject":"icm","domain":"ICM","codes":["2_IC_C_3"],"note":"ards-ventilation","refs":[],"status":"draft","stem":"<p>A patient with moderate–severe ARDS has a P/F ratio of 15 kPa despite optimised PEEP and lung-protective ventilation.</p>\n<p>Which intervention has been shown to reduce mortality in this group?</p>","options":["High-frequency oscillatory ventilation","High-dose steroids on day 1 for all patients","Inhaled nitric oxide","Prone positioning for at least 16 hours a day","Routine surfactant"],"answer":3,"explain":"<p><strong>Prone positioning</strong> (≥ 16 h/day) in moderate–severe ARDS (P/F &lt; 20 kPa) reduced mortality in the PROSEVA trial. Inhaled NO improves oxygenation transiently without a mortality benefit; HFOV was harmful in one large trial.</p>"},{"id":"fq-icm-003","subject":"icm","domain":"ICM","codes":["2_IC_C_3"],"note":"ards-ventilation","refs":[],"status":"draft","stem":"<p>A patient ventilated for severe asthma becomes profoundly hypotensive, with rising plateau pressures and a silent chest.</p>\n<p>What is the immediate management?</p>","options":["Disconnect the ventilator briefly to allow exhalation and reduce the respiratory rate","Increase PEEP to 15 cmH<sub>2</sub>O","Increase respiratory rate to clear CO<sub>2</sub>","Give a 1 L fluid bolus only","Start dobutamine"],"answer":0,"explain":"<p>This suggests <strong>dynamic hyperinflation</strong> (gas trapping) raising intrathoracic pressure and reducing venous return. <strong>Disconnect</strong> to let the chest deflate, then ventilate with a <strong>low rate and long expiratory time</strong> (accepting hypercapnia); consider tension pneumothorax.</p>"},{"id":"fq-icm-004","subject":"icm","domain":"ICM","codes":["2_IC_E_1"],"note":"sepsis-organ-support","refs":[],"status":"draft","stem":"<p>A patient with septic shock remains hypotensive (MAP 58 mmHg) after 30 mL/kg of balanced crystalloid.</p>\n<p>What is the recommended first-line vasopressor?</p>","options":["Adrenaline","Dobutamine","Dopamine","Noradrenaline","Phenylephrine"],"answer":3,"explain":"<p>The Surviving Sepsis Campaign recommends <strong>noradrenaline</strong> as the first-line vasopressor to a MAP ≥ 65 mmHg, adding <strong>vasopressin</strong> rather than escalating noradrenaline further, and hydrocortisone for ongoing requirements.</p>"},{"id":"fq-icm-005","subject":"icm","domain":"ICM","codes":["2_IC_C_6"],"note":"aki-rrt-icu","refs":[],"status":"draft","stem":"<p>Continuous veno-venous haemofiltration (CVVH) removes solutes mainly by which mechanism?</p>","options":["Active transport","Convection","Diffusion","Osmosis","Ultrafiltration of cells"],"answer":1,"explain":"<p><strong>Haemofiltration</strong> removes solutes by <strong>convection</strong> (solvent drag across a membrane with replacement fluid), which is relatively better for middle molecules. <strong>Haemodialysis</strong> uses <strong>diffusion</strong> across a concentration gradient.</p>"},{"id":"fq-icm-006","subject":"icm","domain":"ICM","codes":["2_IC_C_6"],"note":"aki-rrt-icu","refs":[],"status":"draft","stem":"<p>A patient on continuous renal replacement therapy with regional citrate anticoagulation develops a rising total calcium, a falling ionised calcium and a widening total-to-ionised calcium ratio.</p>\n<p>What is the most likely explanation?</p>","options":["Citrate accumulation (reduced metabolism, e.g. liver failure)","Hypercalcaemia of malignancy","Hyperparathyroidism","Inadequate citrate dose","Normal finding"],"answer":0,"explain":"<p>Citrate binds calcium; if <strong>citrate is not metabolised</strong> (liver failure, poor perfusion), citrate–calcium complexes accumulate: total calcium rises while ionised calcium falls (ratio &gt; about 2.5), often with metabolic acidosis. Reduce citrate or change anticoagulation.</p>"},{"id":"fq-icm-007","subject":"icm","domain":"ICM","codes":["2_IC_C_11"],"note":"aki-rrt-icu","refs":[],"status":"draft","stem":"<p>A malnourished patient in ICU starts enteral feeding. Two days later he develops weakness, arrhythmias and respiratory failure.</p>\n<p>Which electrolyte abnormality is most characteristic?</p>","options":["Hypercalcaemia","Hyperkalaemia","Hypernatraemia","Hypophosphataemia","Hypermagnesaemia"],"answer":3,"explain":"<p><strong>Refeeding syndrome</strong>: insulin release drives phosphate (and potassium and magnesium) into cells, causing <strong>hypophosphataemia</strong>, with muscle weakness, respiratory failure and arrhythmias. Start feeding slowly, supplement electrolytes and give thiamine.</p>"},{"id":"fq-icm-008","subject":"icm","domain":"ICM","codes":["2_IC_C_5"],"note":"icm-neuro-haem-endocrine","refs":[],"status":"draft","stem":"<p>A patient with Guillain–Barré syndrome has a vital capacity that has fallen from 40 to 16 mL/kg over 12 hours, with difficulty swallowing.</p>\n<p>What is the most appropriate management?</p>","options":["Continue observation on the ward","Elective intubation and ventilation in ICU","High-flow nasal oxygen and discharge planning","Non-invasive ventilation as definitive support","Start pyridostigmine"],"answer":1,"explain":"<p>A <strong>rapidly falling vital capacity</strong> (below about 15–20 mL/kg) with <strong>bulbar dysfunction</strong> indicates impending respiratory failure: plan <strong>elective intubation</strong> in ICU (avoid suxamethonium — hyperkalaemia). NIV is unsuitable with bulbar weakness.</p>"},{"id":"fq-icm-009","subject":"icm","domain":"ICM","codes":["2_IC_C_10"],"note":"icm-neuro-haem-endocrine","refs":[],"status":"draft","stem":"<p>A patient on unfractionated heparin after cardiac surgery has a fall in platelet count from 250 to 80 × 10<sup>9</sup>/L on day 7 with a new DVT.</p>\n<p>What is the most likely diagnosis and immediate action?</p>","options":["DIC; give platelets","Dilutional thrombocytopenia; observe","Heparin-induced thrombocytopenia; stop all heparin and start a non-heparin anticoagulant (e.g. argatroban)","ITP; give steroids","Sepsis; give antibiotics only"],"answer":2,"explain":"<p>A &gt; 50% platelet fall at <strong>5–10 days</strong> with <strong>thrombosis</strong> is typical of <strong>HIT</strong> (high 4Ts score). Stop all heparin (including flushes), start a non-heparin anticoagulant, send antibody testing; avoid platelet transfusion unless bleeding.</p>"},{"id":"fq-icm-010","subject":"icm","domain":"ICM","codes":["2_GA_O_3"],"note":"ecmo-advanced","refs":[],"status":"draft","stem":"<p>A patient on peripheral veno-arterial ECMO via the femoral vessels has good oxygenation in the right radial arterial gas but upper body cyanosis and a low right radial PaO<sub>2</sub> after lung function deteriorates.</p>\n<p>What is this phenomenon?</p>","options":["Harlequin (differential hypoxaemia) syndrome","Recirculation","Oxygenator failure only","Pulmonary embolism","Methaemoglobinaemia"],"answer":0,"explain":"<p>With <strong>femoral VA-ECMO</strong>, oxygenated circuit blood flows retrogradely up the aorta, while the recovering heart ejects poorly oxygenated blood from failing lungs into the aortic root, supplying the <strong>coronary and cerebral circulations</strong>: <strong>differential hypoxaemia (Harlequin syndrome)</strong>. Monitor right radial gases; options include adding a venous return cannula (V-AV).</p>"},{"id":"fq-icm-011","subject":"icm","domain":"ICM","codes":["2_IC_J_1"],"note":"end-of-life-donation","refs":[],"status":"draft","stem":"<p>After brainstem death has been confirmed, a potential organ donor develops polyuria of 400 mL/h with rising plasma sodium.</p>\n<p>What is the most likely cause and treatment?</p>","options":["Cerebral salt wasting; give saline","Diabetes insipidus; give desmopressin or vasopressin and replace water","Hyperglycaemia; give insulin only","Mannitol effect; observe","SIADH; restrict fluids"],"answer":1,"explain":"<p>Loss of posterior pituitary function after brainstem death causes <strong>diabetes insipidus</strong>: dilute polyuria and hypernatraemia. Treat with <strong>desmopressin</strong> (or vasopressin, which also supports blood pressure) and water replacement to protect organs for donation.</p>"},{"id":"fq-icm-012","subject":"icm","domain":"ICM","codes":["2_IC_I_1"],"note":"end-of-life-donation","refs":[],"status":"draft","stem":"<p>A family asks whether stopping ventilation is legally different from never having started it.</p>\n<p>Which statement is correct in UK law and professional guidance?</p>","options":["Withdrawing treatment is illegal once started","Withdrawing treatment requires a court order","There is no legal or ethical difference between withholding and withdrawing treatment that is not in the patient's best interests","Only relatives can decide to withdraw treatment","Withdrawal is only permitted after brainstem death"],"answer":2,"explain":"<p>UK law and GMC guidance treat <strong>withholding and withdrawing</strong> as ethically and legally equivalent when treatment is no longer in the patient's best interests. Decisions are made by the senior clinician, involving the patient (where possible) and those close to them.</p>"},{"id":"fq-pa-001","subject":"pain","domain":"PA","codes":["2_PA_D_1"],"note":"neuropathic-cancer-pain","refs":[],"status":"draft","stem":"<p>A patient has burning pain and allodynia in the distribution of a nerve injured during surgery 4 months ago.</p>\n<p>Which medication is a recommended first-line option for neuropathic pain?</p>","options":["Codeine","Duloxetine","Oral morphine sustained-release","Paracetamol","Tramadol for long-term use"],"answer":1,"explain":"<p>NICE recommends <strong>amitriptyline, duloxetine, gabapentin or pregabalin</strong> as first-line for neuropathic pain. Tramadol is only for acute rescue; strong opioids have limited benefit and significant risks.</p>"},{"id":"fq-pa-002","subject":"pain","domain":"PA","codes":["2_PA_C_2"],"note":"chronic-pain","refs":[],"status":"draft","stem":"<p>For chronic primary pain, which medication does NICE NG193 suggest may be considered?</p>","options":["An antidepressant such as duloxetine or amitriptyline","Gabapentin","Long-term oral morphine","Paracetamol","Regular NSAIDs"],"answer":0,"explain":"<p>For <strong>chronic primary pain</strong>, NICE says an <strong>antidepressant</strong> may be considered (after discussion), and advises <strong>against</strong> starting opioids, gabapentinoids, NSAIDs, paracetamol, benzodiazepines or local anaesthetics.</p>"},{"id":"fq-pa-003","subject":"pain","domain":"PA","codes":["2_PA_C_7"],"note":"neuropathic-cancer-pain","refs":[],"status":"draft","stem":"<p>A patient with pancreatic cancer has severe upper abdominal pain radiating to the back despite high-dose opioids.</p>\n<p>Which interventional procedure is most appropriate to consider?</p>","options":["Coeliac plexus neurolysis","Epidural steroid injection","Lumbar sympathetic block","Stellate ganglion block","Trigger point injections"],"answer":0,"explain":"<p><strong>Coeliac plexus neurolysis</strong> (e.g. with alcohol) is effective for pain from <strong>pancreatic and upper abdominal malignancy</strong>, reducing opioid requirements.</p>"},{"id":"fq-pa-004","subject":"pain","domain":"PA","codes":["2_PA_F"],"note":"acute-pain-complex","refs":[],"status":"draft","stem":"<p>Which operation is associated with one of the highest rates of chronic post-surgical pain?</p>","options":["Carpal tunnel release","Cataract surgery","Laparoscopic cholecystectomy","Limb amputation","Tonsillectomy in adults"],"answer":3,"explain":"<p><strong>Amputation</strong> (phantom and stump pain), thoracotomy and mastectomy have among the highest rates of persistent post-surgical pain (often 30–50% or more for amputation).</p>"},{"id":"fq-pa-005","subject":"pain","domain":"PA","codes":["2_PA_B_4","2_PA_D_2"],"note":"acute-pain-complex","refs":[],"status":"draft","stem":"<p>A patient on 16 mg/day sublingual buprenorphine for opioid dependence needs an emergency laparotomy.</p>\n<p>Which is the most appropriate analgesic approach?</p>","options":["Stop buprenorphine and use naloxone","Continue buprenorphine, maximise regional and non-opioid analgesia, and add full agonist opioids as needed with monitoring and specialist advice","No analgesia is needed because buprenorphine is analgesic","Switch to tramadol only","Use pethidine infusion"],"answer":1,"explain":"<p>Current practice usually <strong>continues buprenorphine</strong> (to avoid withdrawal and relapse), uses <strong>regional and multimodal non-opioid analgesia</strong>, and adds <strong>full agonist opioids</strong> as needed (higher doses may be required) with monitoring and acute pain/addiction team input.</p>"},{"id":"fq-pa-006","subject":"pain","domain":"PA","codes":["2_PA_C_1"],"note":"neuropathic-cancer-pain","refs":[],"status":"draft","stem":"<p>A patient has persistent pain, swelling, colour and temperature change and sweating in the hand after a wrist fracture, disproportionate to the injury.</p>\n<p>What is the most likely diagnosis?</p>","options":["Carpal tunnel syndrome","Complex regional pain syndrome type I","Complex regional pain syndrome type II","Compartment syndrome","Gout"],"answer":1,"explain":"<p><strong>CRPS</strong> (Budapest criteria: sensory, vasomotor, sudomotor/oedema and motor/trophic changes) after injury <strong>without a confirmed major nerve injury</strong> is <strong>type I</strong>; type II follows a nerve injury.</p>"},{"id":"fq-pa-007","subject":"pain","domain":"PA","codes":["2_PA_B_3"],"note":"acute-pain-complex","refs":[],"status":"draft","stem":"<p>A patient with opioid-induced hyperalgesia after major surgery has escalating pain despite increasing morphine doses.</p>\n<p>Which adjunct is most appropriate to consider?</p>","options":["Doubling the morphine dose","Low-dose ketamine infusion","Midazolam infusion","Stopping all analgesia","Topical capsaicin"],"answer":1,"explain":"<p><strong>Opioid-induced hyperalgesia</strong> involves NMDA-mediated central sensitisation. A <strong>low-dose ketamine</strong> infusion (with monitoring) and opioid reduction or rotation, plus regional techniques, can help.</p>"},{"id":"fq-pa-008","subject":"pain","domain":"PA","codes":["2_PA_C_4","2_PA_D_1"],"note":"neuropathic-cancer-pain","refs":[],"status":"draft","stem":"<p>What is the mechanism of action of gabapentin and pregabalin?</p>","options":["μ-opioid receptor agonism","Binding to the α<sub>2</sub>δ subunit of voltage-gated calcium channels","GABA<sub>A</sub> receptor agonism","NMDA receptor antagonism","Sodium channel blockade"],"answer":1,"explain":"<p>Despite their names, gabapentinoids do not act on GABA receptors: they bind the <strong>α<sub>2</sub>δ subunit of voltage-gated calcium channels</strong>, reducing excitatory transmitter release in the dorsal horn.</p>"},{"id":"fq-ps-001","subject":"professional","domain":"PS","codes":["2_PS_A_3","2_PS_A_1"],"note":"sedation-advanced","refs":[],"status":"draft","stem":"<p>Which sedative is most likely to produce rousable sedation with minimal respiratory depression?</p>","options":["Dexmedetomidine","Midazolam","Propofol","Remifentanil","Thiopental"],"answer":0,"explain":"<p><strong>Dexmedetomidine</strong> (an α<sub>2</sub>-agonist) produces sleep-like, rousable sedation with little respiratory depression; bradycardia and hypotension are its main side effects.</p>"},{"id":"fq-ps-002","subject":"professional","domain":"PS","codes":["2_PS_D_1","2_PS_D_2"],"note":"sedation-advanced","refs":[],"status":"draft","stem":"<p>A patient with severe OSA, a BMI of 50 and a full stomach needs a prolonged ERCP in the prone position.</p>\n<p>What is the most appropriate choice?</p>","options":["Deep propofol sedation with a nasal cannula","General anaesthesia with a secured airway","Midazolam boluses by the endoscopist","No sedation","Oral premedication only"],"answer":1,"explain":"<p>Sedation is <strong>inappropriate</strong> when the airway is high-risk (severe OSA, obesity), aspiration risk is high, and the procedure is long and prone. <strong>General anaesthesia with a secured airway</strong> is safer.</p>"},{"id":"fq-ps-003","subject":"professional","domain":"PS","codes":["2_PS_B_2"],"note":"sedation-advanced","refs":[],"status":"draft","stem":"<p>According to current UK monitoring standards, which monitor should be used during deep procedural sedation in addition to pulse oximetry and blood pressure?</p>","options":["Bispectral index only","Continuous waveform capnography","Oesophageal temperature","Peripheral nerve stimulator","Transcutaneous CO<sub>2</sub> only if the procedure is longer than 2 hours"],"answer":1,"explain":"<p><strong>Capnography</strong> detects airway obstruction and hypoventilation earlier than pulse oximetry and is recommended for <strong>moderate and deep sedation</strong> (Association of Anaesthetists 2021).</p>"},{"id":"fq-pro-001","subject":"professional","domain":"PRO","codes":["2_SQI_C"],"note":"qi-safety-stage2","refs":[],"status":"draft","stem":"<p>A run chart of weekly theatre start times shows a single week that is much later than all others, then a return to the usual pattern.</p>\n<p>What does this most likely represent?</p>","options":["A process improvement","Common-cause variation requiring a system redesign","A special-cause signal worth investigating","Seasonal trend","Normal variation that should be ignored completely"],"answer":2,"explain":"<p>An <strong>astronomical point</strong> far outside the usual pattern is a <strong>special-cause</strong> signal — worth investigating (e.g. an equipment failure). Treating every fluctuation as special (tampering) is unhelpful, but clear outliers warrant review.</p>"},{"id":"fq-pro-002","subject":"professional","domain":"PRO","codes":["2_RMD_G_1"],"note":"research-appraisal","refs":[],"status":"draft","stem":"<p>A large trial finds that a new drug reduces postoperative nausea scores by 0.2 points on a 10-point scale (p = 0.001).</p>\n<p>What is the most appropriate interpretation?</p>","options":["The result is clinically important because p is very small","The result is statistically significant but probably not clinically important","The result is not statistically significant","The trial must have been underpowered","The drug should be adopted immediately"],"answer":1,"explain":"<p>A very small p value reflects a large sample, not a large effect. A 0.2-point change is unlikely to be <strong>clinically meaningful</strong> (compare with the minimal clinically important difference).</p>"},{"id":"fq-pro-003","subject":"professional","domain":"PRO","codes":["2_RMD_G_5","2_RMD_G_6"],"note":"research-appraisal","refs":[],"status":"draft","stem":"<p>In a trial, patients whose anaesthetist knew their allocation received more rescue analgesia in the control group, although the protocol said rescue analgesia should be given for the same pain scores.</p>\n<p>Which bias is most likely?</p>","options":["Attrition bias","Performance bias from lack of blinding","Publication bias","Recall bias","Selection bias from lack of randomisation"],"answer":1,"explain":"<p>When clinicians know the allocation, their care may differ systematically: <strong>performance bias</strong>. Blinding of participants and clinicians reduces it.</p>"},{"id":"fq-pro-004","subject":"professional","domain":"PRO","codes":["2_SQI_H_3"],"note":"sustainability-medication","refs":[],"status":"draft","stem":"<p>Which inhalational agent has the highest global warming potential per unit of use in typical clinical practice?</p>","options":["Desflurane","Isoflurane","Oxygen","Sevoflurane","Xenon"],"answer":0,"explain":"<p><strong>Desflurane</strong> has a GWP<sub>100</sub> of about 2,500 and is used at high concentrations, making its climate impact far greater than sevoflurane's. N<sub>2</sub>O also has a large impact (long atmospheric life and high volumes).</p>"},{"id":"fq-pro-005","subject":"professional","domain":"PRO","codes":["2_SG_D_1"],"note":"professional-leadership","refs":[],"status":"draft","stem":"<p>A patient with severe depression who lacks capacity to consent is recommended ECT by the psychiatrist.</p>\n<p>Which framework governs whether ECT can be given?</p>","options":["ECT can never be given without consent","The anaesthetist decides alone","Mental health and mental capacity legislation with specific safeguards (e.g. second opinion), respecting any valid advance refusal","The family's consent alone is sufficient","The Children Act 1989"],"answer":2,"explain":"<p>ECT without consent requires the safeguards of <strong>mental health and mental capacity law</strong> (for example a second-opinion doctor), and a <strong>valid advance decision to refuse</strong> ECT must be respected except in specific emergencies. The anaesthetist must be satisfied the legal basis is in place.</p>"},{"id":"fq-pro-006","subject":"professional","domain":"PRO","codes":["2_SQI_J_2"],"note":"qi-safety-stage2","refs":[],"status":"draft","stem":"<p>A patient suffers a dental injury during a difficult intubation.</p>\n<p>Which action best fulfils the professional duty of candour?</p>","options":["Say nothing unless the patient complains","Tell the patient what happened, apologise, explain the next steps and document the conversation","Tell only the surgeon","Wait for the incident investigation before telling the patient","Ask the ward nurse to inform the patient"],"answer":1,"explain":"<p>The <strong>duty of candour</strong> requires openness and honesty: tell the patient promptly, <strong>apologise</strong>, explain what happened and what will be done (e.g. dental review), provide support, and document it; report the incident.</p>"},{"id":"fq-pom-001","subject":"pom","domain":"POM","codes":["2_POM_G_1"],"note":"cpet-risk","refs":[],"status":"draft","stem":"<p>A 72-year-old is referred for CPET before an open colectomy. His anaerobic threshold is 8.5 mL/kg/min and peak VO<sub>2</sub> is 12 mL/kg/min.</p>\n<p>How should these results be interpreted?</p>","options":["Low risk; no change to plans","Higher perioperative risk; discuss with the patient, consider prehabilitation and plan for critical care","The test is invalid without a stress echo","Surgery is absolutely contraindicated","The results only predict cardiac events"],"answer":1,"explain":"<p>An <strong>AT below about 10–11 mL/kg/min</strong> and peak VO<sub>2</sub> below about 15 mL/kg/min are associated with <strong>higher postoperative morbidity and mortality</strong> after major abdominal surgery. Use the results in <strong>shared decision-making</strong>, consider <strong>prehabilitation</strong>, and plan the level of postoperative care.</p>"},{"id":"fq-pom-002","subject":"pom","domain":"POM","codes":["2_POM_F_2"],"note":"cpet-risk","refs":[],"status":"draft","stem":"<p>Which is a recognised limitation of the P-POSSUM score?</p>","options":["It cannot be used for emergency surgery","It includes no physiological variables","It overestimates mortality in low-risk patients and requires intraoperative data","It predicts only cardiac events","It is designed for children"],"answer":2,"explain":"<p>P-POSSUM includes <strong>operative variables</strong> (so cannot be fully calculated beforehand) and tends to <strong>overpredict</strong> mortality in low-risk patients. Scores should inform, not replace, clinical judgement.</p>"},{"id":"fq-pom-003","subject":"pom","domain":"POM","codes":["2_POM_O_1"],"note":"cardiac-risk-assessment","refs":[],"status":"draft","stem":"<p>A patient had a drug-eluting stent inserted 3 months ago for stable angina and takes aspirin and clopidogrel. He is listed for an elective inguinal hernia repair.</p>\n<p>What is the most appropriate plan?</p>","options":["Proceed and stop both antiplatelets 7 days before","Defer elective surgery until dual antiplatelet therapy can be safely reduced (usually at least 6 months after elective PCI), after discussion with cardiology","Stop aspirin and continue clopidogrel","Switch to warfarin bridging","Proceed with both drugs and a spinal anaesthetic"],"answer":1,"explain":"<p>Stopping DAPT early risks <strong>stent thrombosis</strong> (with high mortality). For non-urgent surgery, <strong>defer</strong> until DAPT can be safely shortened (typically ≥ 6 months after elective PCI; 12 months after ACS), with cardiology input. Neuraxial block on clopidogrel is contraindicated.</p>"},{"id":"fq-pom-004","subject":"pom","domain":"POM","codes":["2_POM_D_7","2_IC_C_4"],"note":"cardiac-risk-assessment","refs":[],"status":"draft","stem":"<p>A patient with hypertrophic obstructive cardiomyopathy becomes hypotensive and tachycardic after induction.</p>\n<p>Which treatment is most appropriate?</p>","options":["Dobutamine infusion","Glyceryl trinitrate","Isoprenaline","Phenylephrine and fluid","Salbutamol"],"answer":3,"explain":"<p>In <strong>HOCM</strong>, LVOT obstruction worsens with <strong>tachycardia, reduced preload and afterload, and increased contractility</strong>. Treat with <strong>fluids and a pure α-agonist</strong> (phenylephrine), and β-blockade for tachycardia; avoid inotropes and vasodilators.</p>"},{"id":"fq-pom-005","subject":"pom","domain":"POM","codes":["2_POM_D_2"],"note":"cardiac-risk-assessment","refs":[],"status":"draft","stem":"<p>An adult with a Fontan circulation needs laparoscopic surgery.</p>\n<p>Which factor most threatens their cardiac output?</p>","options":["A modest rise in heart rate","High intrathoracic pressure and pneumoperitoneum reducing passive pulmonary blood flow","Mild hypercapnia at 5.5 kPa","Spontaneous ventilation","Use of a peripheral cannula"],"answer":1,"explain":"<p>In a <strong>Fontan circulation</strong>, systemic venous blood flows <strong>passively</strong> to the lungs, depending on the gradient between systemic venous pressure and pulmonary/left atrial pressures. <strong>Raised intrathoracic pressure</strong> (high PEEP, IPPV), pneumoperitoneum, hypovolaemia and raised PVR all reduce flow and cardiac output.</p>"},{"id":"fq-pom-006","subject":"pom","domain":"POM","codes":["2_POM_O_2","2_POM_D_6"],"note":"anticoag-anaemia-pom","refs":[],"status":"draft","stem":"<p>A patient listed for colorectal cancer surgery in 3 weeks has Hb 98 g/L, ferritin 12 µg/L and transferrin saturation 8%.</p>\n<p>What is the most appropriate management?</p>","options":["Oral iron and proceed regardless of response","Intravenous iron now","Preoperative blood transfusion to Hb 120 g/L","Erythropoietin alone","No treatment; anaemia does not affect outcomes"],"answer":1,"explain":"<p><strong>Iron deficiency anaemia</strong> before major surgery within a few weeks is best treated with <strong>IV iron</strong> (faster than oral), reducing transfusion and possibly complications. Transfusion should be reserved for symptomatic or severe anaemia.</p>"},{"id":"fq-pom-007","subject":"pom","domain":"POM","codes":["2_POM_O_1"],"note":"anticoag-anaemia-pom","refs":[],"status":"draft","stem":"<p>A patient with atrial fibrillation (CHA<sub>2</sub>DS<sub>2</sub>-VASc 3) takes apixaban and has normal renal function. She is to have a total hip replacement under spinal anaesthesia.</p>\n<p>What is the usual preoperative plan?</p>","options":["Bridge with therapeutic LMWH after stopping apixaban","Continue apixaban until the morning of surgery","Stop apixaban about 72 hours before a neuraxial block (per local guidance), without bridging","Stop apixaban 12 hours before","Switch to warfarin"],"answer":2,"explain":"<p>DOACs do not need bridging. For a <strong>neuraxial block</strong> and high-bleeding-risk surgery, apixaban is usually stopped about <strong>72 hours</strong> beforehand with normal renal function (check the current guideline and local policy), and restarted when haemostasis allows.</p>"},{"id":"fq-pom-008","subject":"pom","domain":"POM","codes":["2_POM_D_5","2_IC_C_7"],"note":"renal-liver-pom","refs":[],"status":"draft","stem":"<p>A patient who had a heart transplant 5 years ago becomes bradycardic (38 beats per minute) and hypotensive under anaesthesia.</p>\n<p>Which drug is least likely to be effective?</p>","options":["Adrenaline","Atropine","Isoprenaline","Transcutaneous pacing","Ephedrine with a direct-acting component"],"answer":1,"explain":"<p>The <strong>transplanted heart is denervated</strong>, so <strong>atropine</strong> (which works by blocking vagal tone) has little effect. Use <strong>directly acting</strong> β-agonists (isoprenaline, adrenaline) or pacing.</p>"},{"id":"fq-pom-009","subject":"pom","domain":"POM","codes":["2_IC_C_7"],"note":"renal-liver-pom","refs":[],"status":"draft","stem":"<p>A patient with cirrhosis has bilirubin 55 µmol/L, albumin 26 g/L, INR 1.9, moderate ascites and mild encephalopathy.</p>\n<p>What is the Child–Pugh class?</p>","options":["Class A","Class B","Class C","Cannot be calculated without creatinine","Class D"],"answer":2,"explain":"<p>Points: bilirubin 34–50 = 2, &gt; 50 = <strong>3</strong>; albumin &lt; 28 = <strong>3</strong>; INR 1.7–2.3 = <strong>2</strong>; moderate ascites = <strong>3</strong>; mild encephalopathy = <strong>2</strong>. Total <strong>13 = Class C</strong> (10–15), indicating high perioperative risk.</p>"},{"id":"fq-pom-010","subject":"pom","domain":"POM","codes":["2_POM_D_1"],"note":"respiratory-neuro-pom","refs":[],"status":"draft","stem":"<p>A 19-year-old with Duchenne muscular dystrophy needs surgery for scoliosis.</p>\n<p>Which anaesthetic drug is contraindicated?</p>","options":["Propofol","Remifentanil","Rocuronium in reduced doses with monitoring","Suxamethonium","Sugammadex"],"answer":3,"explain":"<p>In muscular dystrophies, <strong>suxamethonium</strong> can cause <strong>hyperkalaemic cardiac arrest and rhabdomyolysis</strong>; volatile agents are also generally avoided (rhabdomyolysis). TIVA is preferred; cardiomyopathy and restrictive lung disease need careful assessment.</p>"},{"id":"fq-pom-011","subject":"pom","domain":"POM","codes":["2_POM_D_1"],"note":"respiratory-neuro-pom","refs":[],"status":"draft","stem":"<p>A patient with generalised myasthenia gravis taking pyridostigmine is to have laparoscopic surgery.</p>\n<p>Which statement about neuromuscular blockers is correct?</p>","options":["Myasthenic patients are resistant to rocuronium","Myasthenic patients are sensitive to non-depolarising blockers and relatively resistant to suxamethonium","Neostigmine is contraindicated because it will cause a myasthenic crisis","Sugammadex is ineffective in myasthenia","Suxamethonium doses should be halved"],"answer":1,"explain":"<p>Fewer functional acetylcholine receptors make patients <strong>sensitive to non-depolarising NMBs</strong> (use small doses and quantitative monitoring; sugammadex is effective) and <strong>resistant to suxamethonium</strong> (higher doses needed).</p>"},{"id":"fq-pom-012","subject":"pom","domain":"POM","codes":["2_POM_H_2","2_POM_G_6"],"note":"respiratory-neuro-pom","refs":[],"status":"draft","stem":"<p>A patient has a STOP-BANG score of 6 and an apnoea–hypopnoea index of 42 on a sleep study. He uses CPAP at home.</p>\n<p>Which perioperative plan is most appropriate for a laparoscopic cholecystectomy?</p>","options":["Heavy benzodiazepine premedication","Bring CPAP to hospital and use it postoperatively, minimise opioids with multimodal and regional analgesia, extubate awake and sitting up","Routine postoperative ventilation overnight","Stop CPAP for 48 hours after surgery","Long-acting opioid infusion without monitoring"],"answer":1,"explain":"<p>AHI &gt; 30 is <strong>severe OSA</strong>. Continue <strong>CPAP</strong> perioperatively, use <strong>opioid-sparing</strong> analgesia, avoid sedative premedication, extubate fully awake in a semi-upright position, and monitor postoperatively according to risk.</p>"},{"id":"fq-pom-013","subject":"pom","domain":"POM","codes":["2_POM_D_8"],"note":"endocrine-disease-pom","refs":[],"status":"draft","stem":"<p>A patient with acromegaly is listed for trans-sphenoidal pituitary surgery.</p>\n<p>Which airway problem is most characteristic?</p>","options":["Atlantoaxial instability","Macroglossia, prognathism and laryngeal narrowing leading to difficult mask ventilation and intubation","Microstomia","Tracheal deviation from goitre only","Tracheomalacia after radiotherapy"],"answer":1,"explain":"<p>Excess growth hormone causes <strong>macroglossia</strong>, prognathism, thickened soft tissues and <strong>narrowed glottis/subglottis</strong>, with OSA. Plan for difficult mask ventilation and intubation (video laryngoscopy, awake techniques).</p>"},{"id":"fq-pom-014","subject":"pom","domain":"POM","codes":["2_POM_D_3"],"note":"haematology-pom","refs":[],"status":"draft","stem":"<p>A patient previously treated with bleomycin for a testicular tumour needs a laparotomy.</p>\n<p>Which intraoperative measure is recommended?</p>","options":["Avoid neuromuscular blockers","Use 100% oxygen throughout to protect the lungs","Use the lowest inspired oxygen concentration that maintains adequate saturation","Use nitrous oxide","Use large tidal volumes"],"answer":2,"explain":"<p>Bleomycin sensitises the lungs to <strong>oxygen toxicity</strong> (pneumonitis, ARDS). Use the <strong>lowest FiO<sub>2</sub></strong> that maintains adequate oxygenation, careful fluid balance, and protective ventilation.</p>"},{"id":"fq-pom-015","subject":"pom","domain":"POM","codes":["2_POM_D_7"],"note":"haematology-pom","refs":[],"status":"draft","stem":"<p>A patient with sickle cell anaemia (HbSS, Hb 80 g/L) is scheduled for laparoscopic cholecystectomy.</p>\n<p>What is the most appropriate preoperative transfusion plan?</p>","options":["No transfusion is ever needed","Transfuse to raise Hb to about 100 g/L, after haematology discussion","Exchange transfusion to HbS &lt; 1% for all cases","Transfuse to Hb 150 g/L","Give erythropoietin only"],"answer":1,"explain":"<p>For medium-risk surgery in HbSS, <strong>preoperative top-up transfusion to about 100 g/L</strong> reduces complications such as acute chest syndrome (TAPS trial). Plan with haematology; avoid hypoxia, dehydration, hypothermia and acidosis.</p>"},{"id":"fq-ra-001","subject":"regional","domain":"RA","codes":["2_RA_A_2"],"note":"regional-upper-limb","refs":[],"status":"draft","stem":"<p>A patient with severe COPD (FEV<sub>1</sub> 0.9 L) needs arthroscopic shoulder surgery.</p>\n<p>Which regional technique is least suitable because of its effect on respiratory function?</p>","options":["General anaesthesia with local infiltration by the surgeon","Standard-volume interscalene brachial plexus block","Suprascapular and axillary nerve blocks","Superior trunk block with low volume (discussed with the patient)","Surgeon-placed subacromial catheter"],"answer":1,"explain":"<p>A standard <strong>interscalene</strong> block causes <strong>ipsilateral phrenic nerve palsy</strong> in nearly all patients, reducing FVC by about 25–30%, which may be poorly tolerated with severe COPD. Diaphragm-sparing alternatives (suprascapular ± axillary nerve blocks, low-volume superior trunk block) reduce this risk.</p>"},{"id":"fq-ra-002","subject":"regional","domain":"RA","codes":["2_RA_E_2"],"note":"regional-lower-limb","refs":[],"status":"draft","stem":"<p>After a total knee replacement, which block provides analgesia while best preserving quadriceps strength for early mobilisation?</p>","options":["Adductor canal block","Femoral nerve block","Fascia iliaca block","Lumbar plexus block","Obturator nerve block"],"answer":0,"explain":"<p>The <strong>adductor canal block</strong> targets the saphenous nerve (and nerve to vastus medialis), providing knee analgesia with <strong>much less quadriceps weakness</strong> than femoral block, supporting mobilisation and reducing falls.</p>"},{"id":"fq-ra-003","subject":"regional","domain":"RA","codes":["2_RA_B_2"],"note":"chest-wall-blocks","refs":[],"status":"draft","stem":"<p>An 82-year-old with five unilateral rib fractures and a platelet count of 90 × 10<sup>9</sup>/L on apixaban (last dose 12 hours ago) has poor analgesia and falling SpO<sub>2</sub>.</p>\n<p>Which regional technique is most appropriate?</p>","options":["Erector spinae plane catheter","Intercostal nerve blocks at every level","Interpleural catheter","Thoracic epidural","Thoracic paravertebral catheter"],"answer":0,"explain":"<p>Recent anticoagulation contraindicates <strong>thoracic epidural</strong> and makes deep techniques (paravertebral) less attractive. An <strong>erector spinae plane catheter</strong> is superficial, compressible and low-risk, and provides useful analgesia for rib fractures as part of multimodal care.</p>"},{"id":"fq-ra-004","subject":"regional","domain":"RA","codes":["2_RA_B_3"],"note":"chest-wall-blocks","refs":[],"status":"draft","stem":"<p>Which statement about the transversus abdominis plane (TAP) block is correct?</p>","options":["It provides reliable visceral analgesia for laparotomy","It involves injecting local anaesthetic between internal oblique and transversus abdominis to block somatic abdominal wall nerves","It is performed in the epidural space","It blocks the phrenic nerve","It is contraindicated in children"],"answer":1,"explain":"<p>The TAP block deposits local anaesthetic <strong>between internal oblique and transversus abdominis</strong>, blocking the anterior rami of T7–L1 (depending on approach). It provides <strong>somatic</strong> abdominal wall analgesia only, not visceral.</p>"},{"id":"fq-ra-005","subject":"regional","domain":"RA","codes":["2_RA_E_2"],"note":"regional-lower-limb","refs":[],"status":"draft","stem":"<p>Which block targets the articular branches of the femoral and accessory obturator nerves to the anterior hip capsule, aiming to spare quadriceps strength?</p>","options":["Adductor canal","Fascia iliaca (infra-inguinal)","iPACK","PENG (pericapsular nerve group) block","Popliteal sciatic"],"answer":3,"explain":"<p>The <strong>PENG block</strong> targets articular branches between the iliopubic eminence and the psoas tendon, providing hip analgesia with less motor block than femoral or fascia iliaca blocks in many reports.</p>"},{"id":"fq-ra-006","subject":"regional","domain":"RA","codes":["2_RA_C_1"],"note":"regional-upper-limb","refs":[],"status":"draft","stem":"<p>During an ultrasound-guided block, a nerve appears bright and fascicular at one probe angle but almost disappears when the probe is tilted slightly.</p>\n<p>What is this phenomenon?</p>","options":["Acoustic enhancement","Anisotropy","Bayonet artefact","Mirror image artefact","Reverberation"],"answer":1,"explain":"<p><strong>Anisotropy</strong>: the echogenicity of nerves and tendons depends on the angle of insonation. Tilting the probe (to make the beam perpendicular to the nerve) optimises its visibility.</p>"},{"id":"fq-ra-007","subject":"regional","domain":"RA","codes":["2_RA_E_7","2_SQI_F_1"],"note":"ra-safety","refs":[],"status":"draft","stem":"<p>What is the purpose of the \"Stop Before You Block\" check?</p>","options":["To check the patient is fasted","To confirm the local anaesthetic dose","To confirm the correct site and side immediately before needle insertion, preventing wrong-sided blocks","To confirm the patient has consented to surgery","To document the block afterwards"],"answer":2,"explain":"<p>\"Stop Before You Block\" is a brief pause <strong>immediately before needle insertion</strong> to re-confirm the <strong>site and side</strong>, because time can elapse between the WHO sign-in and the block. Wrong-sided blocks are never events.</p>"},{"id":"fq-ra-008","subject":"regional","domain":"RA","codes":["2_RA_E_5","2_RA_A_3"],"note":"regional-upper-limb","refs":[],"status":"draft","stem":"<p>Which adjunct most reliably prolongs the analgesic duration of a single-shot brachial plexus block, and is effective whether given perineurally or intravenously?</p>","options":["Adrenaline","Dexamethasone","Fentanyl","Midazolam","Sodium bicarbonate"],"answer":1,"explain":"<p><strong>Dexamethasone</strong> prolongs block duration by several hours, with similar effect <strong>perineurally and intravenously</strong> in many studies. Adrenaline modestly prolongs short-acting agents and reduces absorption; bicarbonate speeds onset.</p>"},{"id":"fq-ra-009","subject":"regional","domain":"RA","codes":["2_RA_E_1"],"note":"ra-safety","refs":[],"status":"draft","stem":"<p>An orthopaedic surgeon is concerned about a regional block for a patient with a tibial shaft fracture because of compartment syndrome.</p>\n<p>Which approach is most appropriate?</p>","options":["Never use regional anaesthesia in lower limb trauma","Use a dense long-acting block without discussion","Discuss with the surgeon, consider lower concentrations and a plan for monitoring (including compartment pressure measurement), and document","Use a block but hide it from the surgical team","Give a spinal with intrathecal morphine only"],"answer":2,"explain":"<p>Regional analgesia can be used in limb trauma with a shared plan: discuss the risk, use <strong>lower concentrations</strong> to avoid dense blocks, ensure <strong>monitoring</strong> (clinical and compartment pressures), and document. Ischaemic pain usually breaks through; surveillance is the key.</p>"},{"id":"fq-ra-010","subject":"regional","domain":"RA","codes":["2_RA_E_6"],"note":"paeds-common-surgery","refs":[],"status":"draft","stem":"<p>A caudal block with 0.2% levobupivacaine is planned for a 12 kg child having an orchidopexy.</p>\n<p>Which volume is typically used for a lumbar/sacral block height?</p>","options":["0.1 mL/kg","0.5 mL/kg","1 mL/kg","2 mL/kg","5 mL/kg"],"answer":2,"explain":"<p>Caudal volumes (Armitage): about <strong>0.5 mL/kg</strong> for sacral dermatomes, <strong>1 mL/kg</strong> for lumbosacral spread (e.g. inguinal and testicular surgery) and about 1.25 mL/kg for lower thoracic spread, always within the maximum dose. Here 12 mL of 0.2% = 24 mg = <strong>2 mg/kg</strong>, at the usual maximum.</p>"},{"id":"fq-ra-011","subject":"regional","domain":"RA","codes":["2_RA_D_3"],"note":"ophthalmic-anaesthesia","refs":[],"status":"draft","stem":"<p>Which block technique for cataract surgery uses a blunt cannula to reach the space around the globe and has a low risk of globe perforation?</p>","options":["Peribulbar block","Retrobulbar block","Sub-Tenon's block","Topical anaesthesia with intracameral injection","Van Lint block"],"answer":2,"explain":"<p><strong>Sub-Tenon's block</strong> delivers local anaesthetic beneath Tenon's capsule with a <strong>blunt cannula</strong>, avoiding sharp needles near the globe; serious complications are rare compared with needle blocks.</p>"},{"id":"fq-ra-012","subject":"regional","domain":"RA","codes":["2_RA_B_4"],"note":"chest-wall-blocks","refs":[],"status":"draft","stem":"<p>Which nerves must be blocked for analgesia after an open inguinal hernia repair?</p>","options":["Femoral and obturator nerves","Iliohypogastric, ilioinguinal and genital branch of the genitofemoral nerve","Lateral cutaneous nerve of the thigh only","Pudendal nerve","T4–T6 intercostal nerves"],"answer":1,"explain":"<p>The inguinal region is supplied by the <strong>iliohypogastric and ilioinguinal nerves (L1)</strong> and the <strong>genital branch of the genitofemoral nerve</strong>; block these (ultrasound-guided or field block) with local infiltration.</p>"}];
